QB.add(39,[{"id":39001,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A blood donor is found on screening to have an active infection, but has never noticed any symptoms. How is this infection classified by its symptoms?","o":["Subclinical (inapparent)","Apparent, because the infection is active and detectable in the blood","Latent, because no symptoms have appeared at any stage","Acute, because it was found suddenly on a single screening test","Primary, because it is the first time it has been detected"],"e":"Infections are classified by the causative agent and by symptoms. Apparent infections produce symptoms; inapparent, silent or subclinical infections are active but produce no noticeable symptoms; latent infections are inactive or dormant. An active infection with no symptoms is subclinical.","w":["Apparent infections produce symptoms.","Latent infections are inactive or dormant, whereas this one is active.","Acute means sudden, rapid onset of disease, not detection on a test.","Primary describes the first infection of a host, not the symptom category."],"k":"Active without symptoms = subclinical; dormant = latent.","rn":[["","Active infection with no noticeable symptoms may be called?","Active infection with no noticeable symptoms may be called?","Silent; Subclinical"]]},{"id":39002,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which pairing of infection type and description is correct?","o":["Latent: inactive or dormant","Apparent: active but produces no noticeable symptoms in the host","Subclinical: inactive and dormant within specific host cells","Latent: produces obvious symptoms throughout the infection","Apparent: dormant until reactivated by ageing"],"e":"By symptoms, infections are apparent (produces symptoms), inapparent/silent/subclinical (active, no noticeable symptoms) or latent (inactive or dormant).","w":["That describes a subclinical infection.","That describes a latent infection.","Latent infections are dormant, without symptoms.","Apparent infections produce symptoms; dormancy is latency."],"k":"Apparent = symptoms; subclinical = active, silent; latent = dormant.","rn":[["","An inactive or dormant infection is called?","An inactive or dormant infection is called?","Latent infection"]]},{"id":39003,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which sequence correctly lists the six links of the chain of infection?","o":["Causative agent → reservoir → portal of exit → mode of transmission → portal of entry → susceptible host","Reservoir → causative agent → portal of entry → mode of transmission → portal of exit → susceptible host","Susceptible host → portal of exit → reservoir → causative agent → mode of transmission → portal of entry","Causative agent → portal of entry → reservoir → mode of transmission → portal of exit → susceptible host","Mode of transmission → causative agent → reservoir → susceptible host → portal of exit → portal of entry"],"e":"A series of events must happen for germs (bacteria, fungi, viruses) to cause infection: causative agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, susceptible host, which then leads back to a new cycle. Each part is a separate link, and breaking any one link stops infection.","w":["The agent comes before the reservoir, and exit precedes entry.","The host is the last link, not the first.","The agent must exit the reservoir before it can enter a new host.","The chain starts with the causative agent."],"k":"C-R-E-M-E-S: agent, reservoir, exit, mode, entry, susceptible host.","rn":[["","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Mode of transmission; Portal of entry; Susceptible host"],["","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Causative agent; Reservoir; Portal of exit"]]},{"id":39004,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Why is the chain of infection a useful concept for infection control?","o":["Breaking any one link stops infection","Infection can only be stopped by treating the susceptible host with antibiotics","All six links must be broken at the same time to have any effect","It shows that infection depends only on the causative agent","It applies only to viral infections spread by aerosols"],"e":"Each part of the process is a separate link in the chain. Break any one link and infection is stopped. Control measures can therefore target the agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry or the susceptible host.","w":["Measures can target any link, not just the host.","Breaking a single link is enough.","Infection depends on all six links, not just the agent.","The chain applies to bacteria, fungi and viruses and all routes."],"k":"Breaking any single link stops infection.","rn":[["","How can infection be stopped using the chain of infection?","How can infection be stopped using the chain of infection?","Break any one link"],["","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Mode of transmission; Portal of entry; Susceptible host"],["","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Causative agent; Reservoir; Portal of exit"]]},{"id":39005,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"In this diagram of the chain of infection, which label marks the link immediately after the reservoir?","o":["3","2","4","5","6"],"e":"The labels show: 1 causative agent; 2 reservoir; 3 portal of exit; 4 mode of transmission; 5 portal of entry; 6 susceptible host. Follow the arrows clockwise from the causative agent and back round to it.","w":["2 is the reservoir itself.","4 is the mode of transmission, which follows the portal of exit.","5 is the portal of entry.","6 is the susceptible host."],"k":"After the reservoir comes the portal of exit.","f":"cp07","rn":[["","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Causative agent; Reservoir; Portal of exit"]]},{"id":39006,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A 78-year-old develops shingles decades after childhood chickenpox. Which statement best explains this?","o":["An endogenous latent virus reactivated as immunosurveillance declined","A new exogenous virus infected the patient from a contact","A bacterial commensal became an opportunistic pathogen","A vertical infection passed on at birth finally became symptomatic","The virus was transmitted through the germ line"],"e":"Most viral diseases are due to exogenous viruses, but some result from reactivation of an endogenous virus latent within specific host cells, such as shingles (VZV), recurrent oral and genital herpes (HSV) and CMV infections. Immunosurveillance controls these latent viruses, and ageing or immunosuppression can lead to reactivation.","w":["Shingles is reactivation of an endogenous latent virus, not a new exogenous infection.","Shingles is caused by VZV, a virus, not a bacterial commensal.","Childhood chickenpox, not birth, was the source.","Germ-line transmission is via the genome, which does not describe shingles."],"k":"Shingles = reactivation of endogenous latent VZV with declining immunosurveillance.","rn":[["","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","Lies latent in cells; Reactivates later"],["","What are 3 reactivated endogenous viruses?","What are 3 reactivated endogenous viruses?","Shingles (VZV); Recurrent herpes (HSV); CMV infections"]]},{"id":39007,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as an example of reactivation of an endogenous latent virus?","o":["Common cold from rhinovirus","Shingles from VZV","Recurrent oral herpes from HSV","Recurrent genital herpes from HSV","CMV infections"],"e":"The speaker notes list shingles (VZV), recurrent oral and genital herpes (HSV) and CMV infections as examples of endogenous viruses reactivating from latency. The common cold from rhinovirus is given as an example of a human respiratory tract reservoir, not latent reactivation.","w":["Shingles from VZV is listed.","Recurrent oral herpes from HSV is listed.","Recurrent genital herpes from HSV is listed.","CMV infections are listed."],"k":"Reactivation examples: VZV (shingles), HSV, CMV.","rn":[["","Which disease comes from the the human respiratory tract reservoir virus?","Which disease comes from the the human respiratory tract reservoir virus?","Common cold"]]},{"id":39008,"t":"Chain of Infection","a":["endogenous","endogenous infection"],"s":"What term describes an infection caused by an agent already present inside the host, as opposed to one acquired from outside?","e":"Infections can be endogenous (from an agent already inside the host) or exogenous (from outside the host). Reactivation of latent viruses such as VZV, HSV and CMV are endogenous examples.","k":"Endogenous = from inside the host; exogenous = from outside.","rn":[["","Which 2 origins can the causative agent have?","Which 2 origins can the causative agent have?","Endogenous; Exogenous"],["","What are 3 reactivated endogenous viruses?","What are 3 reactivated endogenous viruses?","Shingles (VZV); Recurrent herpes (HSV); CMV infections"]]},{"id":39009,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A gardener develops tetanus after a puncture wound contaminated with soil. What is the reservoir of the causative agent?","o":["The environment (soil)","An animal reservoir in cattle","The human gut","Water systems","The human respiratory tract"],"e":"A reservoir is where the agent survives, grows and/or multiplies, and can be human, animal or environmental. The slide gives soil as the environmental reservoir for Clostridium tetani, which causes tetanus.","w":["Cattle are the reservoir for toxigenic E. coli.","The human gut is the reservoir for rotavirus.","Water is the reservoir for Legionella pneumophila.","The human respiratory tract is the reservoir for rhinovirus."],"k":"Clostridium tetani: soil reservoir.","rn":[["","Soil is the reservoir for which organism?","Soil is the reservoir for which organism?","Clostridium tetani"]]},{"id":39010,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which pairing of reservoir, microorganism and disease is correct?","o":["Water; Legionella pneumophila; Legionnaires' disease","Soil; Salmonella spp.; gastroenteritis","Poultry; Clostridium tetani; tetanus","Cow; rhinovirus; common cold","Human gut; Legionella pneumophila; Legionnaires' disease"],"e":"The reservoir table: soil, Clostridium tetani, tetanus; water, Legionella pneumophila, Legionnaires' disease; cow, toxigenic E. coli, gastroenteritis; poultry, Salmonella spp., gastroenteritis; human respiratory tract, rhinovirus, common cold; human gut, rotavirus, gastroenteritis.","w":["Salmonella's reservoir is poultry.","C. tetani's reservoir is soil.","Rhinovirus's reservoir is the human respiratory tract.","Legionella's reservoir is water."],"k":"Water → Legionella; soil → C. tetani; poultry → Salmonella.","rn":[["","Water is the reservoir for which organism?","Water is the reservoir for which organism?","Legionella pneumophila"],["","Which disease comes from the water reservoir organism?","Which disease comes from the water reservoir organism?","Legionnaires' disease"]]},{"id":39011,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Several people develop gastroenteritis after eating undercooked chicken. Which reservoir and organism best fit the lecture's examples?","o":["Poultry; Salmonella spp.","Soil; Clostridium tetani","Water; Legionella pneumophila","Human respiratory tract; rhinovirus","Cow; toxigenic E. coli"],"e":"Animal reservoirs in the lecture are cows (toxigenic Escherichia coli causing gastroenteritis) and poultry (Salmonella spp. causing gastroenteritis). Undercooked chicken fits the poultry reservoir and Salmonella.","w":["C. tetani causes tetanus from soil.","Legionella causes Legionnaires' disease from water.","Rhinovirus causes the common cold.","Toxigenic E. coli is linked to cows, not chicken."],"k":"Poultry is the reservoir for Salmonella gastroenteritis.","rn":[["","Poultry is the reservoir for which organism?","Poultry is the reservoir for which organism?","Salmonella spp."]]},{"id":39012,"t":"Chain of Infection","a":["reservoir","a reservoir"],"s":"What is the term for the place where an infectious agent survives, grows and/or multiplies?","e":"The reservoir is the place where the agent survives, grows and/or multiplies. It can be human, animal or environmental, and it is the second link in the chain of infection.","k":"Reservoir = where the agent survives and multiplies.","rn":[["","What is a reservoir of infection?","What is a reservoir of infection?","Where the agent; Survives, grows, multiplies"]]},{"id":39013,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which pairing of portal of exit and what carries the microbe out is correct?","o":["Urethra: urine","Eyes: ear wax","Anus: tears","Mammary glands: faeces","Skin: semen"],"e":"The worksheet lists: nose (secretions), mouth (saliva, sputum), mammary glands (milk, secretions), vagina (secretions, blood), urethra (urine), ear (ear wax), eyes (tears), broken skin (blood), skin (flakes), anus (faeces), seminal vesicles (semen).","w":["The eyes shed tears; ear wax comes from the ear.","The anus sheds faeces; tears come from the eyes.","Mammary glands shed milk and secretions.","Skin sheds flakes; semen comes from the seminal vesicles."],"k":"Each portal of exit has its own vehicle (e.g. urethra → urine).","rn":[["","Routine screening: 2 fluid cultures?","Routine screening: 2 fluid cultures?","Blood cultures; Urine culture"]]},{"id":39014,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which portal of exit is linked to blood as the vehicle carrying microbes out of the body?","o":["Broken skin","Intact skin, which sheds microbes on flakes","The ear, which sheds microbes in ear wax","The eyes, which shed microbes in tears","The urethra, which sheds microbes in urine"],"e":"The portals of exit table lists broken skin with blood as the vehicle (the vagina is also listed with secretions and blood). Intact skin sheds flakes, the ear ear wax, the eyes tears and the urethra urine.","w":["Intact skin sheds flakes.","The ear sheds ear wax.","The eyes shed tears.","The urethra sheds urine."],"k":"Broken skin: blood as the exit vehicle.","rn":[["","What carries microbes out through broken skin?","What carries microbes out through broken skin?","Blood"]]},{"id":39015,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"This body diagram shows red arrows entering and leaving at each surface. Which entry route is shown that does NOT involve a body surface opening?","o":["An arthropod bite into a capillary","The conjunctiva of the eye","The respiratory tract","The alimentary tract","The urinogenital tract"],"e":"The body-surfaces figure labels conjunctiva, respiratory tract, alimentary tract, urinogenital tract, anus and skin as sites of infection and shedding, plus entry by an arthropod bite into a capillary and by a scratch or injury. An arthropod bite delivers organisms directly into a capillary rather than through a natural opening.","w":["The conjunctiva is a body surface site.","The respiratory tract is a body surface site.","The alimentary tract is a body surface site.","The urinogenital tract is a body surface site."],"k":"Entry routes include arthropod bites and injuries as well as body surfaces.","f":"ws01"},{"id":39016,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A healthcare worker sustains a needlestick injury from a patient's used needle. Which mode of transmission is this?","o":["Inoculation","Ingestion through the gastrointestinal tract","Droplet spread through the respiratory tract","Transplacental spread from mother to foetus","Airborne spread by aerosols over long distances"],"e":"The modes of transmission are contact (direct or indirect from contaminated objects), droplet, airborne, ingestion (gastrointestinal tract), inoculation (accidental sharps injury or bites) and transplacental (across the placenta from mother to foetus). A sharps injury is inoculation.","w":["Ingestion is via the gastrointestinal tract.","Droplet is inhaled through the respiratory tract.","Transplacental spread crosses the placenta.","Airborne spread uses aerosols."],"k":"Sharps injuries and bites = inoculation.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways inoculation transmission happens?","What are the 2 ways inoculation transmission happens?","Accidental sharps injury; Bites"]]},{"id":39017,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts droplet and airborne transmission?","o":["Droplets are >5 µm and fall within about 2 m; aerosols are <5 µm and travel further","Droplets are <5 µm and travel far; aerosols are >5 µm and fall within 2 m","Both fall to the ground within about 2 m","Both travel more than 2 m and float for hours","Droplets need negative pressure rooms; aerosols need only surgical masks"],"e":"Droplets are large, usually >5 µm, and fall to the ground rapidly, within about 2 m; they often contaminate objects, generating fomites, and are easier to contain. Airborne aerosols are <5 µm, can float in the air for hours and travel significantly more than 2 m, and are harder to contain, needing negative pressure rooms and N95 respirators.","w":["This reverses the sizes and distances.","Aerosols travel further than 2 m.","Droplets fall within about 2 m.","Negative pressure and N95 masks are for airborne spread."],"k":"Droplet >5 µm, <2 m; airborne <5 µm, >2 m.","rn":[["","Droplet vs airborne: distance travelled","Droplet vs airborne: distance travelled","Droplet: falls within ~2 m; Airborne: significantly >2 m"]]},{"id":39018,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which statement about droplet versus airborne spread is NOT correct?","o":["Airborne transmission is easier to control than droplet transmission","Airborne transmission travels further than droplet transmission","Droplet transmission can lead to spread via fomites","Droplet transmission is easier to control","Airborne control may need negative pressure rooms"],"e":"The worksheet MCQ marks as true: airborne travels further than droplet; droplet can lead to transmission via fomites; droplet is easier to control. \"Airborne is easier to control\" is not correct: aerosols are harder to contain and may need negative pressure rooms and N95 masks.","w":["Airborne aerosols do travel further.","Droplets do contaminate objects, creating fomites.","Droplet spread is easier to control.","Negative pressure rooms are used for airborne spread."],"k":"Droplet spread is easier to control than airborne.","rn":[["","Droplet vs airborne: which is easier to contain?","Droplet vs airborne: which is easier to contain?","Droplet"]]},{"id":39019,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"In this figure, the bottom panel shows viruses settling on surfaces, being touched and carried to the nose or mouth. Which route is this?","o":["Fomite transmission","Close-contact spray at short range","Airborne transmission at a distance","Transplacental transmission","Inoculation by a sharps injury"],"e":"The figure shows three routes from an infected person releasing viruses by expiration and evaporation: close contact (spray or inhalation nearby), airborne (viruses stay in room air and are inhaled at a distance) and fomite (viruses settle on surfaces by deposition, are picked up by touch and carried to the nose or mouth, or are stirred back into the air by resuspension).","w":["Close contact is the top panel, with direct spray nearby.","Airborne spread is the middle panel.","Transplacental spread is mother to foetus.","Inoculation is via sharps or bites."],"k":"Fomite route: deposition on surfaces → touch → nose/mouth.","f":"cp10","rn":[["","How does the fomite route spread infection?","How does the fomite route spread infection?","Viruses on surfaces; Hand touches, then nose/mouth"]]},{"id":39020,"t":"Chain of Infection","a":["fomite","fomites"],"s":"What term describes a contaminated object that spreads infection indirectly?","e":"A fomite is a contaminated object that spreads infection. Droplets often contaminate objects, generating fomites, and indirect contact transmission occurs from contaminated objects.","k":"Fomite = contaminated object spreading infection.","rn":[["","What is a fomite?","What is a fomite?","Contaminated object that spreads infection"]]},{"id":39021,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which pairing of organism and portal of entry is given in the lecture?","o":["Tuberculosis: respiratory tract","Salmonella: respiratory tract","Diphtheria: gastrointestinal tract","Tuberculosis: broken skin","Salmonella: urethra"],"e":"The slide gives tuberculosis and diphtheria as examples where the portal of entry is the respiratory tract, and Salmonella as entering through the gastrointestinal tract.","w":["Salmonella enters through the gastrointestinal tract.","Diphtheria enters through the respiratory tract.","TB enters through the respiratory tract.","Salmonella enters through the gut."],"k":"TB and diphtheria: respiratory entry; Salmonella: GI entry.","rn":[["","What is the portal of entry for tuberculosis and diphtheria?","What is the portal of entry for tuberculosis and diphtheria?","Respiratory tract"]]},{"id":39022,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a risk factor that accelerates infection in a host?","o":["Regular exercise and a balanced diet","Very young or very old age","Open wounds and invasive procedures","A suppressed immune system","A large number of infectious microorganisms"],"e":"Worksheet 1 lists risk factors: age (the very young and very old), nutrition and sociocultural condition, open wounds and invasive procedures, a suppressed immune system or weak health, and the presence and/or number of infectious microorganisms. Exercise and a balanced diet are measures to protect the host.","w":["Extremes of age are a listed risk factor.","Open wounds and invasive procedures are listed.","Immunosuppression is listed.","The number of microorganisms is listed."],"k":"Host risk: age extremes, nutrition, wounds, immunosuppression, microbial load.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways to maintain the healing process?","What are the 3 ways to maintain the healing process?","Balanced diet; Appetite of patient; Reduce stress"]]},{"id":39023,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Why do hospital patients acquire infections more easily than people in the community?","o":["Invasive procedures, immunosuppressive drugs and lapses in infection control","Hospitals contain no commensal organisms at all","Hospital air is always free of pathogens","Patients are protected from pathogens by antibiotics in every case","Microorganisms cannot survive in health care settings"],"e":"Worksheet 1 lists surgery, invasive procedures and devices; immunosuppressive drugs; microorganisms flourishing in health care settings, helped by breaks in infection-control procedures; and patients' weakened defence mechanisms, which set the stage for nosocomial infections.","w":["Hospitals contain many organisms.","Hospital air is not pathogen-free.","Antibiotics do not protect every patient.","Microorganisms flourish in health care settings."],"k":"Hospital risk: procedures, devices, immunosuppression, infection-control lapses.","rn":[["","Why do hospital patients get infections easily? (treatment)","Why do hospital patients get infections easily? (treatment)","Surgery, procedures, devices; Immunosuppressive drugs"]]},{"id":39024,"t":"Chain of Infection","a":["susceptible host","the susceptible host"],"s":"What is the final link in the chain of infection: a person who can become infected by an infectious agent?","e":"The susceptible host is a person who can become infected by an infectious agent. Various factors affect susceptibility, including age, nutrition, wounds, immunosuppression and microbial load.","k":"The susceptible host is the last link.","rn":[["","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Mode of transmission; Portal of entry; Susceptible host"],["","What is a susceptible host?","What is a susceptible host?","Someone who can be infected"]]},{"id":39025,"t":"Transmission Routes","s":"A baby acquires an infection from its mother during pregnancy. Which transmission route term applies?","o":["Vertical","Horizontal, because both mother and baby are human","Zoonotic, because the infection crosses between individuals","Iatrogenic, because pregnancy care is a medical setting","Nosocomial, because the baby was born in hospital"],"e":"The transmission routes are horizontal (between members of the same species), vertical (from parent to offspring), zoonotic (between members of different species), nosocomial (acquired in hospital or health care), iatrogenic (via a medical procedure or treatment) and germ-line (via the genome).","w":["Horizontal is between members of the same species, not parent to offspring.","Zoonotic is between different species.","Iatrogenic is via a medical procedure or treatment.","Nosocomial is acquired in hospital."],"k":"Parent to offspring = vertical transmission.","rn":[["","What is vertical transmission?","What is vertical transmission?","From parent to offspring"]]},{"id":39026,"t":"Transmission Routes","s":"A patient develops an infection directly caused by a contaminated instrument used during a procedure. Which term fits best?","o":["Iatrogenic","Zoonotic, spread between members of different species","Vertical, spread from parent to offspring","Germ-line, spread through the genome","Horizontal, between unrelated people in the community"],"e":"Iatrogenic transmission happens via a medical procedure or treatment. Nosocomial means acquired in hospital or health care more generally. Zoonotic is between species, vertical is parent to offspring, and germ-line is via the genome.","w":["Zoonotic is between different species.","Vertical is parent to offspring.","Germ-line is via the genome.","This was caused by a procedure, not community spread."],"k":"Infection via a medical procedure = iatrogenic.","rn":[["","What is iatrogenic transmission?","What is iatrogenic transmission?","Via medical procedure / treatment"]]},{"id":39027,"t":"Transmission Routes","a":["zoonotic","zoonosis","zoonotic transmission"],"s":"What term describes transmission between members of different species?","e":"Zoonotic transmission is between members of different species. Viral haemorrhagic fever such as Ebola is mainly a zoonosis, with possible fruit bat and bushmeat sources.","k":"Zoonotic = between different species.","rn":[["","What is zoonotic transmission?","What is zoonotic transmission?","Between members of different species"]]},{"id":39028,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"According to worksheet 2, which organism is linked to a single route, faeco-oral spread?","o":["Campylobacter spp.","Varicella zoster virus","Staphylococcus aureus","Plasmodium falciparum","Neisseria meningitidis"],"e":"Worksheet 2 links Campylobacter (diarrhoea) to faeco-oral spread. Varicella zoster links to respiratory droplet, fomites, skin contact and airborne; S. aureus to droplet, fomites, skin contact and faeco-oral; P. falciparum to an invertebrate vector and blood; N. meningitidis to respiratory droplet and kissing contact. The examples show some organisms spread in more than one way.","w":["Varicella zoster has several routes, including airborne.","S. aureus has several routes.","Malaria spreads by an invertebrate vector and blood.","N. meningitidis spreads by droplets and kissing contact."],"k":"Campylobacter: faeco-oral; others spread by several routes.","rn":[["","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 other measures?","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 other measures?","Sanitation; Animal husbandry"],["","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 hygiene measures?","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 hygiene measures?","Hand washing; Food hygiene"]]},{"id":39029,"t":"Transmission Routes","s":"Which organism in worksheet 2 is linked to the widest range of routes, including airborne spread?","o":["Varicella zoster (chicken pox)","Neisseria gonorrhoeae (gonorrhoea)","Campylobacter spp. (diarrhoea)","Neisseria meningitidis (meningitis)","Plasmodium falciparum (malaria)"],"e":"Varicella zoster is linked to respiratory droplet, fomites, skin-to-skin contact and airborne spread. N. gonorrhoeae spreads by sexual contact; Campylobacter faeco-orally; N. meningitidis by respiratory droplet and kissing contact; P. falciparum by an invertebrate vector and blood.","w":["Gonorrhoea is linked only to sexual contact.","Campylobacter is linked only to faeco-oral spread.","N. meningitidis is linked to droplet and kissing contact, not airborne.","Malaria is linked to a vector and blood."],"k":"VZV spreads by droplet, fomites, skin contact and airborne routes."},{"id":39030,"t":"Transmission Routes","s":"Which statement about the worksheet 2 organism-and-route list is correct?","o":["It shows that some organisms spread in more than one way","It must be memorised in full for the exam","It shows every organism has exactly one route","It shows that all bacteria are spread by vectors","It shows that viruses never spread by fomites"],"e":"The worksheet notes that the organism examples do not need to be memorised; they show that some organisms spread in more than one way, for example S. aureus by droplet, fomites, skin contact and faeco-oral routes, and varicella zoster by droplet, fomites, skin contact and airborne spread.","w":["The worksheet says the examples need not be memorised.","Several organisms have multiple routes.","Vectors are linked to malaria, not all bacteria.","Varicella zoster is linked to fomites."],"k":"Many organisms use several transmission routes."},{"id":39031,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"An ICU patient has an endotracheal tube removed. The infection-control team sends the tube tip for culture. Which link of the chain of infection is this measure aimed at?","o":["Causative agent","Reservoir, by screening staff with occupational health checks","Portal of exit, by wearing masks and disposable gloves","Portal of entry, by turning and positioning the patient","Susceptible host, by encouraging immunisation"],"e":"Breaking the causative agent link relies on rapid and accurate identification of the organism by routinely screening samples (blood, urine, skin swabs, throat swabs, tracheal aspirate) and equipment cultured after removal (endotracheal tube tip, urinary catheter tip, central line tip), and on controlling or eliminating agents by cleaning, disinfection and sterilisation.","w":["Occupational health measures target the reservoir.","Masks and gloves target the portal of exit.","Turning and positioning target the portal of entry.","Immunisation protects the susceptible host."],"k":"Culturing removed devices identifies the causative agent.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways to break the causative agent link?","What are the 2 ways to break the causative agent link?","Rapid, accurate identification of organism; Control or eliminate infectious agents"],["","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Chain of infection: first 3 links?","Causative agent; Reservoir; Portal of exit"]]},{"id":39032,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"What autoclave conditions are given for sterilisation?","o":["121 °C for 20 minutes","70 °C for 2 minutes with methylated spirits","100 °C for 5 minutes in boiling water","37 °C overnight in an incubator","160 °C for 1 minute in dry air"],"e":"The speaker notes give sterilisation by autoclave at 121 °C for 20 minutes. High-level disinfection uses agents such as 2% activated glutaraldehyde, 1% sodium hypochlorite, 5% carbolic solution and 1% bleach powder; low-level disinfection uses 70% methylated spirits, 10% betadine and 1% savlon.","w":["70% methylated spirits is a low-level disinfectant, not an autoclave setting.","Boiling is not the setting given.","37 °C is not a sterilising temperature.","Dry heat at 160 °C for 1 minute is not the setting given."],"k":"Autoclave: 121 °C for 20 min.","rn":[["","What are the autoclave settings?","What are the autoclave settings?","121°C; For 20 min"]]},{"id":39033,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which agent is listed for LOW-level disinfection?","o":["70% methylated spirits","2% activated glutaraldehyde, used for high-level disinfection","1% sodium hypochlorite solution, a high-level disinfectant","5% carbolic solution, a high-level disinfectant","An autoclave cycle at 121 °C for 20 minutes"],"e":"Low-level disinfection agents in the speaker notes: 70% methylated spirits, 10% betadine solution and 1% savlon. High-level: 2% activated glutaraldehyde, 1% sodium hypochlorite, 5% carbolic solution, 1% bleach powder. Sterilisation: autoclave at 121 °C for 20 min.","w":["Glutaraldehyde is a high-level disinfectant.","Sodium hypochlorite is a high-level disinfectant.","Carbolic solution is a high-level disinfectant.","Autoclaving is sterilisation, not low-level disinfection."],"k":"Low-level: methylated spirits, betadine, savlon.","rn":[["","What are the 3 low level disinfectants?","What are the 3 low level disinfectants?","70% methylated spirits; 10% betadine solution; 1% savlon"]]},{"id":39034,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"A nurse with a communicable illness is restricted from patient contact until recovered. Which link is being targeted?","o":["Reservoir","Portal of entry, by protecting the skin of patients","Mode of transmission, using airborne precautions","Causative agent, by culturing the nurse's equipment","Susceptible host, by improving patient nutrition"],"e":"Controlling the reservoir includes occupational health measures: immunisation and regular check-ups, and restricting workers with a communicable disease from work involving patient contact. Environmental measures include cleaning the hospital with disinfectant and cleaning beds and furniture between patients.","w":["Protecting patients' skin targets the portal of entry.","Airborne precautions target the mode of transmission.","Culturing equipment targets the causative agent.","Nutrition protects the susceptible host."],"k":"Restricting infected staff controls the reservoir.","rn":[["","3 occupational health reservoir controls?","3 occupational health reservoir controls?","Immunisation; Regular check ups; Infected staff: no patient contact"]]},{"id":39035,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which set of measures is listed for breaking the portal of exit?","o":["Aseptic precautions, masks, careful waste handling and disposable gloves","Negative pressure rooms and N95 masks for all of the patients and staff on the ward","Turning and positioning debilitated patients regularly","Immunisation and a balanced diet for every patient","Culturing urinary catheter tips after removal"],"e":"The portal of exit is broken by practising aseptic precautions, wearing masks, careful handling of waste and wearing disposable gloves. Negative pressure and N95 masks are airborne precautions (mode of transmission); turning patients protects the portal of entry; immunisation and diet protect the host; culturing catheter tips identifies the agent.","w":["Negative pressure and N95 masks are airborne transmission precautions.","Turning and positioning protect the portal of entry.","Immunisation and diet protect the host.","Culturing devices identifies the causative agent."],"k":"Portal of exit: asepsis, masks, waste handling, gloves.","rn":[["","Breaking the portal of exit: 2 other measures?","Breaking the portal of exit: 2 other measures?","Aseptic precautions; Careful waste handling"]]},{"id":39036,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"A patient with an airborne infection is admitted. Which precautions match the lecture?","o":["Negative pressure room and N95/P2 masks, limiting movement outside the room","A single room and surgical masks only","Hand hygiene alone, with no room restrictions or masks needed for staff or visitors","Standard bay with curtains drawn and no masks needed","Cleaning non-disposable equipment as the only measure"],"e":"Airborne precautions: negative pressure, N95/P2 masks and limiting patient movement outside the room. Droplet precautions: single room, surgical masks, limit movement. Contact precautions: single room, hand hygiene, PPE (removed when leaving), and cleaning and disinfecting non-disposable equipment.","w":["Single room and surgical masks are droplet precautions.","Hand hygiene alone is insufficient for airborne spread.","A standard bay without masks does not contain aerosols.","Equipment cleaning is part of contact precautions only."],"k":"Airborne: negative pressure + N95/P2.","rn":[["","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","Negative pressure; N95/P2 masks; Limit movement outside room"]]},{"id":39037,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"A patient with MRSA in a wound is placed on contact precautions. Which measure is part of contact precautions?","o":["Removing PPE when leaving the room","Using a negative pressure room for the whole admission","Wearing N95/P2 respirators at all times","Giving prophylaxis to every visitor","Turning the patient every two hours"],"e":"Contact precautions: single patient room; good hand hygiene, PPE as appropriate; remove PPE when leaving; clean and disinfect non-disposable equipment and items. Negative pressure and N95/P2 masks are airborne precautions.","w":["Negative pressure is for airborne precautions.","N95/P2 respirators are for airborne precautions.","Prophylaxis for visitors is not a listed contact precaution.","Turning patients protects the portal of entry."],"k":"Contact precautions include removing PPE on leaving and cleaning equipment.","rn":[["","Contact precautions: PPE and equipment?","Contact precautions: PPE and equipment?","Remove PPE leaving; Disinfect reusable equipment"],["","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Single patient room; Hand hygiene, PPE"]]},{"id":39038,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which measure is aimed at the portal of entry?","o":["Careful handling of catheters and drainage","Restricting staff with communicable disease","Wearing masks to cover the mouth and nose","Encouraging immunisation of patients","Screening blood cultures for organisms"],"e":"Portal of entry measures: maintain integrity of skin and mucous membranes; turn and position debilitated patients; ensure personal hygiene; dispose of contaminated sharps; handle catheters and drainage carefully.","w":["Restricting staff controls the reservoir.","Masks target the portal of exit.","Immunisation protects the susceptible host.","Screening identifies the causative agent."],"k":"Portal of entry: skin integrity, sharps, catheters, hygiene, positioning.","rn":[["","Breaking portal of entry: 2 device measures?","Breaking portal of entry: 2 device measures?","Contaminated sharps disposal; Careful catheters, drainage"]]},{"id":39039,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which set of measures protects the susceptible host?","o":["Oral hygiene, adequate intake, exercise and immunisation","Negative pressure rooms with N95/P2 respirators","Aseptic precautions and careful handling of waste","Culturing the central line tip after it is removed from the patient's neck","Cleaning beds and furniture between patients"],"e":"The susceptible host is protected by oral hygiene, adequate intake, encouraging exercise and immunisation, and the healing process is maintained by a balanced diet, attention to appetite and reducing stress. The other options target the mode of transmission, the portal of exit, the causative agent and the reservoir.","w":["These are airborne precautions (mode of transmission).","These target the portal of exit.","This identifies the causative agent.","This controls the reservoir."],"k":"Protect the host: hygiene, nutrition, exercise, immunisation, reduce stress.","rn":[["","What are the 4 ways to protect the susceptible host?","What are the 4 ways to protect the susceptible host?","Oral hygiene; Adequate intake; Encouraging exercise; Encouraging immunization"]]},{"id":39040,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which pairing of disease, route and measure is correct according to worksheet 2?","o":["Malaria (vertical): prophylaxis in pregnancy","Campylobacter (faeco-oral): bed nets and spraying","HIV (horizontal): food hygiene and cold storage","MRSA: vaccine against the Zaire strain","Malaria (vector): needle exchange programmes"],"e":"Worksheet 2 measures: Campylobacter (faeco-oral) hand washing, food hygiene, sanitation, animal husbandry; HIV (vertical) Caesarean section and breastfeeding as listed; HIV (horizontal) condoms, needle exchange, screening transfusions; malaria (vector) bed nets, vector control, treatment, gametocytocidal drugs; malaria (vertical) prophylaxis in pregnancy; MRSA hand hygiene, isolation, disinfection, sterilisation; Ebola includes a vaccine for the Zaire strain.","w":["Bed nets and spraying are for malaria's mosquito vector.","Food hygiene is for Campylobacter.","The Zaire-strain vaccine is for viral haemorrhagic fever (Ebola).","Needle exchange is for horizontal HIV transmission."],"k":"Match each route to its own measures (e.g. vertical malaria → prophylaxis in pregnancy)."},{"id":39041,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which measures are listed for MRSA spread by hands, fomites and surgical sites?","o":["Hand hygiene, isolation, disinfection and sterilisation","Bed nets, vector control by spraying, and gametocytocidal drugs","Condoms, needle exchange and screening of blood transfusions","Vaccination with the Zaire strain vaccine","Commercial catering practice and cold storage of food"],"e":"Worksheet 2 lists MRSA measures: hand hygiene, isolation, disinfection, sterilisation, public health messages and government targets. The other options belong to malaria, HIV, Ebola and Campylobacter.","w":["Bed nets and gametocytocidal drugs are for malaria.","Condoms and transfusion screening are for HIV.","The Zaire-strain vaccine is for Ebola.","Catering practice and cold storage are for Campylobacter."],"k":"MRSA control: hand hygiene, isolation, disinfection, sterilisation.","rn":[["","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 hygiene measures?","Against faeco-oral Campylobacter: 2 hygiene measures?","Hand washing; Food hygiene"],["","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Single patient room; Hand hygiene, PPE"]]},{"id":39042,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"An anaesthetist intubates a patient with suspected meningococcal disease. Which measure from worksheet 2 protects the anaesthetist?","o":["PPE such as a visor","Bed nets over the patient's bed","Cold storage of hospital food","Caesarean section","Animal husbandry measures"],"e":"For Neisseria meningitidis, spread by kissing contacts and health care procedures, the measures are vaccination, isolation, PPE (visors when intubating), public health messages for early recognition, and prophylaxis.","w":["Bed nets are for malaria vectors.","Cold storage is a food hygiene measure for Campylobacter.","Caesarean section relates to vertical HIV.","Animal husbandry relates to Campylobacter."],"k":"Meningococcus: visors when intubating, vaccination, prophylaxis.","rn":[["","Contact precautions: PPE and equipment?","Contact precautions: PPE and equipment?","Remove PPE leaving; Disinfect reusable equipment"],["","What general measures break the chain of infection?","What general measures break the chain of infection?","Testing; Correct PPE (personal protective equipment); Hand washing"],["","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Contact precautions: room and hygiene?","Single patient room; Hand hygiene, PPE"]]},{"id":39043,"t":"Types of Infection","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts acute and chronic infection?","o":["Acute: sudden onset; chronic: infection continues beyond the time it should have been cleared","Acute: lasts for many years in the host; chronic: lasts only for a few hours before clearance","Acute: no symptoms; chronic: always severe symptoms","Acute: an equilibrium between virus and host; chronic: continual change","Acute and chronic mean the same thing"],"e":"Acute infection is a sudden and rapid onset of disease: the host response and infection change continually until resolution (non-equilibrium). Chronic infection is continued infection by the primary agent for a long period, beyond the time the immune system should have cleared it, with an equilibrium between virus and host until the balance changes.","w":["This reverses the durations.","Symptom severity is not the distinction.","Chronic infection is the equilibrium state.","They are different."],"k":"Acute: sudden, non-equilibrium; chronic: prolonged, equilibrium.","rn":[["","Chronic","Chronic","continued infection by the primary agent for a long period; long beyond when immune system should have cleared it; often following primary infection"]]},{"id":39044,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"Which two mechanisms are listed in the speaker notes for chronic viral infection?","o":["Continuous replication (and latency) and restricted viral gene expression","Rapid lysis of every infected cell followed by complete viral clearance by the immune system","Production of spores and formation of a capsule","Integration into mitochondria and production of endotoxin","Constant fever and continuous diarrhoea"],"e":"The speaker notes give two mechanisms in chronic infection: continuous replication (and latency), and restricted viral gene expression, where no viral proteins are made, so the virus is immunologically silent.","w":["Complete clearance would end the infection.","Spores and capsules are bacterial features not described here.","Endotoxin is a Gram negative bacterial component.","These are symptoms, not mechanisms."],"k":"Chronic: continuous replication or restricted gene expression (immunologically silent).","rn":[["","What are the 2 mechanisms in chronic infection?","What are the 2 mechanisms in chronic infection?","Continuous replication & latency; Restricted viral gene expression"]]},{"id":39045,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"In this graph of virus in tissues over time, which curve shows virus falling to a low level and then flaring again later?","o":["Latent (purple)","Acute (red), with a sharp rise then clearance","Chronic (blue), staying raised for a long time","None of the curves show this pattern","All three curves show this pattern"],"e":"The graph plots the relative amount of virus in tissues against time (months or years). Acute (red) shows a sharp rise then clearance; chronic (blue) shows virus staying at a raised level for a long time; latent (purple) shows virus falling to a low level and then flaring again later (reactivation).","w":["The acute curve rises sharply and clears.","The chronic curve stays raised.","The latent curve shows this pattern.","Only the latent curve reactivates."],"k":"Latent infection: low level then reactivation.","f":"cp26","rn":[["","Latent infection: virus amount?","Latent infection: virus amount?","Peaks early, drops very low; Can rise again later"]]},{"id":39046,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"How does viral DNA persist during latent infection?","o":["As an extrachromosomal element (herpesviruses) or integrated into the host genome (retroviruses)","Only as free RNA in the cytoplasm, which is never copied when the host cell divides","Only as protein, with no nucleic acid remaining","Inside the bacterial cell wall of commensals","As spores in the extracellular space"],"e":"Latent infections are caused by DNA viruses or retroviruses. Viral DNA persists as an extrachromosomal element (herpesviruses) or integrated within the host genome (retroviruses), so during cell division the viral genome is replicated along with the host chromosomes. Retrovirus infection may induce cellular transformation.","w":["Latent viral genomes persist as DNA, copied with the host.","Nucleic acid persists.","Viruses do not hide in bacterial cell walls.","Viruses do not form spores."],"k":"Latency: viral DNA extrachromosomal (herpes) or integrated (retrovirus).","rn":[["","Viral DNA in herpesviruses vs retroviruses: how does it persist?","Viral DNA in herpesviruses vs retroviruses: how does it persist?","Herpesviruses: extrachromosomal element; Retroviruses: integrated into host genome"]]},{"id":39047,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"Which viruses are given as good examples of latent infection?","o":["HIV, and HSV-1 or HSV-2 reactivation","Rhinovirus and rotavirus","Influenza and measles","Legionella pneumophila and Salmonella species","Campylobacter and E. coli"],"e":"Good examples of latent infection are HIV and HSV-1 or HSV-2 reactivation (fever blisters or cold sores). Latent infections are caused by DNA viruses or retroviruses, whose DNA persists in host cells.","w":["Rhinovirus and rotavirus are reservoir examples, not latency examples.","Influenza and measles are not given as latency examples.","Legionella and Salmonella are bacteria.","Campylobacter and E. coli are bacteria."],"k":"Latency examples: HIV, HSV-1/2.","rn":[["","What are 2 good examples of latent infection?","What are 2 good examples of latent infection?","HIV; HSV-1 or HSV-2 reactivation"]]},{"id":39048,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"In the HIV-1 life cycle figure, what happens after the viral RNA genome is reverse transcribed into DNA?","o":["The DNA is integrated into the host DNA, then transcribed or held in latency","The DNA is immediately destroyed by the host cell","The DNA leaves the cell as a spore","The viral RNA is translated directly into protein, without any DNA step at all","The DNA becomes part of the cell wall"],"e":"The HIV-1 figure shows receptor binding and fusion, reverse transcription of the viral genomic RNA into DNA, integration into the host DNA, and then either transcription or latency. Transcription leads to synthesis of viral proteins, assembly and maturation of new virions. The slide shows this for the principle, not the detail.","w":["The DNA is integrated, not destroyed.","Viruses do not form spores.","HIV RNA is reverse transcribed into DNA first.","Human cells do not have cell walls."],"k":"HIV: reverse transcription → integration → transcription or latency.","f":"cp27","rn":[["","HIV-1: after fusion, what happens to its RNA?","HIV-1: after fusion, what happens to its RNA?","Reverse transcribed; Into DNA"]]},{"id":39049,"t":"Timeline of Infection","s":"Which statement correctly defines the incubation period and the latent period?","o":["Incubation: infection to symptoms; latent period: infection to infectiousness","Incubation: infection to infectiousness; latent period: infection to symptoms","Both mean the time from infection to death","Incubation: symptoms to recovery; latent period: recovery to reinfection","Both mean the time a virus stays dormant in neurons"],"e":"The incubation period is the time from infection to onset of symptoms (of disease). The latent period is the time from infection to infectiousness. The terms are sometimes used as synonyms, but a distinction is sometimes made as above, and which is shorter depends on the disease.","w":["This swaps the definitions.","Neither is defined by death.","Neither is defined by recovery.","These are timeline terms, not dormancy in neurons."],"k":"Incubation → symptoms; latent period → infectiousness.","rn":[["","What is the incubation period?","What is the incubation period?","Infection to symptoms"],["","What is the latent period?","What is the latent period?","Time from infection to infectiousness"],["","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Depends on the disease"]]},{"id":39050,"t":"Timeline of Infection","s":"For SARS-CoV-2, people can spread infection before they feel ill. What does this imply about the timeline?","o":["The latent period is shorter than the incubation period","The incubation period is shorter than the latent period","Patients are infectious only during the symptomatic phase","The incubation and latent periods do not apply to viruses","There is no asymptomatic phase"],"e":"In many diseases patients are considered infectious during the symptomatic phase, but this is not always the case, for example with SARS-CoV-2. If the latent period (infection to infectiousness) is shorter than the incubation period (infection to symptoms), people spread infection before they feel ill.","w":["That would mean symptoms appear before infectiousness.","SARS-CoV-2 is the example where this is not true.","These terms apply to viral infections.","An asymptomatic infectious phase exists here."],"k":"Latent period < incubation → spread before symptoms (SARS-CoV-2).","rn":[["","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Depends on the disease"]]},{"id":39051,"t":"Timeline of Infection","s":"In this timeline figure, which label spans from infection until symptoms begin?","o":["1, the incubation period","2, the latent phase only","3, the asymptomatic phase only","4, the symptomatic phase","None; the figure does not show this"],"e":"The timeline shows: 1 the incubation period, spanning the latent and asymptomatic phases until symptoms start; 2 the latent phase; 3 the asymptomatic phase; 4 the symptomatic phase. The box at the top right defines incubation period as the time from infection to onset of symptoms.","w":["The latent phase is only part of the incubation period.","The asymptomatic phase is only part of it.","The symptomatic phase comes after symptoms start.","The figure does show this."],"k":"Incubation period spans the latent and asymptomatic phases.","f":"cp28","rn":[["","What is the incubation period?","What is the incubation period?","Infection to symptoms"],["","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Depends on the disease"]]},{"id":39052,"t":"Timeline of Infection","a":["incubation period","incubation"],"s":"What term describes the time from infection to the onset of symptoms?","e":"The incubation period is the time from infection to onset of symptoms of disease. The latent period is the time from infection to infectiousness.","k":"Incubation period = infection to symptoms.","rn":[["","What is the incubation period?","What is the incubation period?","Infection to symptoms"]]},{"id":39053,"t":"Timeline of Infection","a":["latent period","latency","latent phase"],"s":"What term describes the time from infection to when a person becomes infectious?","e":"The latent period is the time from infection to infectiousness. It is sometimes used as a synonym for incubation period, but a distinction is sometimes made.","k":"Latent period = infection to infectiousness.","rn":[["","What is the latent period?","What is the latent period?","Time from infection to infectiousness"]]},{"id":39054,"t":"Acute, Chronic and Latent Infection","s":"In this outcomes diagram, what can follow the incubation period?","o":["Disease, recurrent illness from latent infection, or asymptomatic infection","Only death, in every case","Only full recovery without any disease","Only a latent infection that never reactivates or causes any disease at all","Only immediate vaccination"],"e":"After the shaded incubation period, infection can lead to disease (which may end in death or recovery and cure), recurrent illnesses (latent infection flaring), or an asymptomatic infection with no disease.","w":["Death is only one possible outcome of disease.","Disease, latency and asymptomatic infection are all possible.","Latent infection can flare as recurrent illness.","Vaccination is not an outcome in this diagram."],"k":"Infection outcomes: disease, recurrent (latent) illness or asymptomatic.","f":"cq26","rn":[["","Outcomes after infection with disease?","Outcomes after infection with disease?","Recovery, cure; Recurrent illness; Death"]]},{"id":39055,"t":"Types of Infection","a":["primary infection","primary"],"s":"What term describes the initial infection of a host by a pathogen?","e":"A primary infection is the initial infection of a host by a pathogen. A latent infection is a persistent infection with no symptoms after primary infection, with symptoms often appearing later.","k":"Primary = first infection of the host.","rn":[["","Primary","Primary","the initial infection of a host by a pathogen."]]},{"id":39056,"t":"Types of Infection","s":"Which statement about latent infection is correct?","o":["It is a persistent, symptom-free infection after primary infection; symptoms often appear later","It is the first infection of a host by any pathogen, before any immune response","It is a sudden, rapid onset of disease","It always clears within a few days","It is caused only by bacteria"],"e":"Latent infection is defined as a persistent infection with no symptoms after primary infection; symptoms often appear some time later. It is caused by DNA viruses or retroviruses, such as HSV and HIV.","w":["That is primary infection.","That is acute infection.","Latent infections persist.","It is caused by DNA viruses or retroviruses."],"k":"Latent = persistent, silent after primary infection, may reactivate.","rn":[["","• Latent","• Latent","A persistent infection that does not have any symptoms after primary infection; Symptoms are often evident sometime later"]]},{"id":39057,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"A ward has an outbreak of diarrhoea from a contaminated shared commode. Which single measure breaks the chain at the reservoir link most directly?","o":["Cleaning and disinfecting the commode between patients","Giving every patient an antibiotic","Placing all patients on the ward in negative pressure rooms","Giving staff N95 respirators","Encouraging patients to exercise"],"e":"Environmental reservoir control includes cleaning the hospital with disinfectant and cleaning beds and furniture between patients. Cleaning and disinfecting shared equipment removes the place where the agent survives. Negative pressure and N95 masks are for airborne spread, and exercise protects the host.","w":["Antibiotics for all patients are not a listed reservoir measure.","Negative pressure is for airborne spread.","N95 respirators are for airborne spread.","Exercise protects the susceptible host."],"k":"Disinfecting shared items controls the reservoir."},{"id":39058,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which measure targets the mode of transmission for droplet-spread infections?","o":["Single room, surgical masks and limiting patient movement","Negative pressure room and N95/P2 masks","Autoclaving at 121 °C for 20 minutes","A balanced diet, attention to appetite and reducing stress","Culturing the urinary catheter tip"],"e":"Droplet precautions: single patient room, surgical masks and limiting patient movement outside the room. Negative pressure and N95/P2 are airborne precautions; autoclaving controls the agent; diet protects the host; culturing devices identifies the agent.","w":["These are airborne precautions.","Autoclaving is sterilisation of the agent.","Diet and stress reduction protect the host.","Culturing identifies the agent."],"k":"Droplet: single room, surgical masks, limit movement.","rn":[["","What are the 3 droplet precautions?","What are the 3 droplet precautions?","Single patient room; Surgical masks; Limit movement outside room"]]},{"id":39059,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which measures are listed for viral haemorrhagic fever such as Ebola?","o":["Fever detection, isolation, antiserum, antivirals and a Zaire-strain vaccine","Bed nets and gametocytocidal drugs","Needle exchange and condoms","Animal husbandry and cold storage","Caesarean section and breastfeeding advice"],"e":"For viral haemorrhagic fever (e.g. Ebola), mainly a zoonosis (possible vector fruit bats, bushmeat) with person-to-person spread in outbreaks, measures are public awareness campaigns, fever detection, isolation, control programmes, antiserum, antivirals and a vaccine (Zaire strain).","w":["Bed nets and gametocytocidal drugs are for malaria.","Needle exchange and condoms are for HIV.","Animal husbandry and cold storage are for Campylobacter.","These relate to vertical HIV."],"k":"Ebola control: awareness, fever detection, isolation, antiserum, antivirals, vaccine."},{"id":39060,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","a":["autoclave","autoclaving","an autoclave"],"s":"Name the piece of equipment used for sterilisation at 121 °C for 20 minutes.","e":"Sterilisation is achieved with an autoclave at 121 °C for 20 minutes, one of the methods for controlling or eliminating infectious agents along with cleaning and disinfection.","k":"Autoclave: sterilisation at 121 °C, 20 min.","rn":[["","What are the autoclave settings?","What are the autoclave settings?","121°C; For 20 min"],["","How is sterilisation done?","How is sterilisation done?","Autoclave"]]},{"id":39061,"t":"Pathogen and Commensals","s":"Which statement correctly distinguishes pathogenicity from virulence?","o":["Pathogenicity is the capacity to cause disease; virulence is its degree","Virulence is the capacity to cause disease; pathogenicity is its degree","Both terms mean the number of organisms in a reservoir","Pathogenicity applies only to viruses and virulence only to bacteria","Virulence is the time from infection to symptoms"],"e":"Pathogenicity is the capacity of an organism to cause disease (the state of being pathogenic). Virulence is the degree of pathogenicity: the organism's ability to bring about that disease. Pathogenicity is yes/no; virulence is how much.","w":["This reverses the definitions.","Neither is about reservoir counts.","Both terms apply to all organisms.","That is the incubation period."],"k":"Pathogenicity = can it cause disease; virulence = how much.","rn":[["","Pathogenicity","Pathogenicity","Capacity of an organism; To cause disease"]]},{"id":39062,"t":"Pathogen and Commensals","s":"Lactobacillus casei lives in the gut, gains nutrients from its host and causes no harm. Which term best describes it?","o":["Commensal","Professional pathogen that almost always causes disease","Opportunistic pathogen of immunocompromised patients","Highly virulent strain of a bacterial pathogen","A reservoir for Legionnaires' disease"],"e":"A commensal is an organism that receives benefit from the host without causing harm. On the spectrum of pathogenicity, Lactobacillus casei and Propionibacterium acnes sit near the commensal end.","w":["Professional pathogens almost always cause disease.","Opportunistic pathogens cause disease only in immunocompromised patients.","It is near the commensal end, not highly virulent.","Legionella's reservoir is water."],"k":"Commensal: benefits from the host without harm.","rn":[["","Commensal","Commensal","Benefits from host; Without causing harm"]]},{"id":39063,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts professional and opportunistic pathogens?","o":["Professional: almost always causes disease; opportunistic: only in immunocompromised patients","Professional: only in immunocompromised patients; opportunistic: almost always causes disease","Both cause disease only in healthy people","Professional pathogens are always viruses; opportunistic ones are always fungi","They are the same thing"],"e":"A professional pathogen almost always causes disease. An opportunistic pathogen only causes disease in immunocompromised patients. Commensals lack the inherent properties of professional pathogens.","w":["This reverses the definitions.","Opportunistic pathogens cause disease in the immunocompromised.","Both groups include various organism types (e.g. S. aureus, HIV, Candida).","They differ."],"k":"Professional = almost always; opportunistic = only if immunocompromised.","rn":[["","What is a professional pathogen?","What is a professional pathogen?","Almost always causes disease"],["","What is an opportunistic pathogen?","What is an opportunistic pathogen?","Only causes disease in immunocompromised"]]},{"id":39064,"t":"Pathogen and Commensals","a":["commensal","a commensal","commensal organism"],"s":"What term describes an organism that receives benefit from the host without causing harm?","e":"A commensal receives benefit from the host without causing harm. Commensal and pathogen are two ends of a spectrum, and virulence is a major factor in where an organism sits.","k":"Commensal = benefit without harm.","rn":[["","Commensal","Commensal","Benefits from host; Without causing harm"]]},{"id":39065,"t":"Pathogen and Commensals","a":["virulence"],"s":"What term describes the degree of pathogenicity of an organism?","e":"Virulence is the degree of pathogenicity caused by an organism, its ability to bring about disease. Pathogenicity is the capacity to cause disease at all.","k":"Virulence = degree of pathogenicity.","rn":[["","Virulence","Virulence","The degree of pathogenicity caused by an organism"]]},{"id":39066,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"On the spectrum of pathogenicity for an immunocompetent person, where do Staphylococcus epidermidis and Candida albicans sit?","o":["In the middle of the spectrum","At the commensal end with Lactobacillus casei","At the pathogen end with HIV and malaria","Off the scale entirely","They are not placed on the spectrum"],"e":"On slide 33, L. casei and P. acnes sit near the commensal end; S. epidermidis and C. albicans in the middle; S. aureus, malaria, HIV, N. meningitidis and S. pneumoniae at the pathogen end.","w":["L. casei and P. acnes are at the commensal end.","HIV and malaria are at the pathogen end.","They are placed on the scale.","They are shown in the middle."],"k":"S. epidermidis and C. albicans sit mid-spectrum in healthy people.","f":"cp33","rn":[["","Which 2 mid-spectrum organisms move to the pathogen end in the immunocompromised?","Which 2 mid-spectrum organisms move to the pathogen end in the immunocompromised?","Staphylococcus epidermidis; Candida albicans"]]},{"id":39067,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"A neutropenic patient on chemotherapy develops a serious Candida albicans infection. Using the lecture's spectrum, which explanation is best?","o":["In immunocompromised patients, low-virulence organisms may become pathogens","C. albicans is a professional pathogen in every person","C. albicans is a commensal and so cannot be pathogenic","Chemotherapy switches on extra virulence genes in C. albicans, turning it into a professional pathogen in everyone","C. albicans only causes disease through the germ line"],"e":"If a patient is immunocompromised, low-virulence organisms may become pathogens: on slide 34 S. epidermidis and C. albicans move to the pathogen end alongside S. aureus, malaria, HIV, N. meningitidis and Streptococcus pyogenes. Pathogenicity depends on virulence, site and the immune state of the patient.","w":["In healthy people C. albicans sits mid-spectrum.","Opportunistic organisms can become pathogens.","The change reflects the host's immune state, not new genes.","Germ-line transmission is unrelated."],"k":"Immunosuppression shifts low-virulence organisms to pathogens.","rn":[["","In immunocompromised patients, low virulence organisms become?","In immunocompromised patients, low virulence organisms become?","Pathogens"]]},{"id":39068,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"Compare this scale with the scale for a healthy person. What has changed?","o":["Every listed organism, including S. epidermidis and C. albicans, now points to the pathogen end","All organisms have moved to the commensal end","The whole scale has been reversed, so that the commensal end is now on the right-hand side","Only HIV has moved, from pathogen to commensal","Nothing has changed between the two scales"],"e":"Slide 34 uses the same scale as slide 33, but in an immunocompromised patient every listed organism, including S. epidermidis and Candida albicans, points to the pathogen end. The commensal organisms drop off the scale.","w":["They move towards, not away from, the pathogen end.","The orientation is unchanged.","HIV remains a pathogen.","The positions change."],"k":"Immunocompromised scale: all listed organisms are pathogens.","f":"cp34","rn":[["","Which 2 mid-spectrum organisms move to the pathogen end in the immunocompromised?","Which 2 mid-spectrum organisms move to the pathogen end in the immunocompromised?","Staphylococcus epidermidis; Candida albicans"]]},{"id":39069,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"Which three things does pathogenicity depend on, according to the lecture?","o":["The organism's virulence, where it is in the body, and the patient's immune state","Only the number of organisms present in the reservoir before transmission occurs","Only the Gram stain result of the organism","The weather, the season and the time of day","The patient's age alone"],"e":"Pathogenicity depends on the organism's virulence, where it is in the body, and the immune state of the patient. Commensals lack the inherent properties of professional pathogens.","w":["Numbers matter for risk, but the three determinants given are virulence, site and immunity.","Gram stain is not a determinant.","These are not listed.","Age is one risk factor, not the determinant list."],"k":"Pathogenicity depends on virulence, site and immune state.","rn":[["","What 3 things does pathogenicity depend on?","What 3 things does pathogenicity depend on?","Organism's virulence; Where it is; Immune state of patient"]]},{"id":39070,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Where does Staphylococcus aureus live as a commensal, and how is it adapted?","o":["In the anterior nares; highly adapted to humans and to the nose","In the stomach; adapted to low pH","In soil, where it is highly adapted to survive long periods of drought and cold","In water systems; adapted to warm pipes","In the bladder; adapted to urine flow"],"e":"S. aureus is a commensal of the anterior nares (front of the nose). It is highly adapted to its species (humans) and to the nose. 20–60% of people are carriers.","w":["The stomach is not its commensal site.","Soil is the reservoir for C. tetani.","Water is the reservoir for Legionella.","The bladder is not described as its commensal site."],"k":"S. aureus: commensal of the anterior nares, adapted to humans.","rn":[["","S. aureus is highly adapted to what 2 things?","S. aureus is highly adapted to what 2 things?","Species; Nose"],["","S. aureus is a commensal of where?","S. aureus is a commensal of where?","Anterior nares"]]},{"id":39071,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"According to the speaker notes, what is the carriage pattern of S. aureus in healthy people?","o":["About 20% persistent, 60% intermittent and 20% almost never carriers","About 90% persistent and 10% never carriers","Everyone carries it permanently","Only 1% of people ever carry it","About 50% of people are persistent carriers and the other 50% are intermittent carriers"],"e":"The slide says 20–60% of people are carriers. The speaker notes say about 20% are persistent carriers, 60% intermittent carriers and about 20% almost never carry it. Worksheet 3 states 30% of the population have S. aureus on their skin, with 10% of this MRSA.","w":["These numbers are not given.","Not everyone carries it.","Carriage is much more common than 1%.","These numbers are not given."],"k":"S. aureus carriage: ~20% persistent, 60% intermittent, 20% never.","rn":[["","In healthy people, what % are persistent, intermittent and never S. aureus carriers?","In healthy people, what % are persistent, intermittent and never S. aureus carriers?","~20% persistent; ~60% intermittent; ~20% almost never"]]},{"id":39072,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"A wound swab grows golden colonies on blood agar, and Gram stain shows Gram positive cocci in clusters. Which organism is most likely?","o":["Staphylococcus aureus","Escherichia coli from an animal reservoir","Legionella pneumophila from a water source","Clostridium tetani from soil","Rhinovirus from the respiratory tract"],"e":"In the lab, S. aureus forms golden colonies on blood agar and appears as Gram positive cocci in clusters (grape-like on the Gram stain). It is also often haemolytic and coagulase positive.","w":["E. coli is a Gram negative rod, not cocci in clusters.","Legionella is not described with golden colonies.","C. tetani is not described with golden colonies and clusters.","Rhinovirus is a virus and does not grow on blood agar."],"k":"S. aureus: golden colonies, Gram positive cocci in clusters.","f":"cp37","rn":[["","Image card: Gram stain of a nasal commensal: what is seen? Which organism?","Gram stain of a nasal commensal: what is seen? Which organism?","Gram positive cocci in clusters; Staphylococcus aureus"],["","Image card: Colonies on blood agar from a nasal swab: what colour? Which organism?","Colonies on blood agar from a nasal swab: what colour? Which organism?","Golden colonies; Staphylococcus aureus"]]},{"id":39073,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which pairing of S. aureus ability and the virulence factors behind it is correct?","o":["Evading immune clearance: protein A and capsule","Adherence to nasal mucosa: cytotoxins and enzymes","Invading tissues: adhesins","Evading immune clearance: adhesins","Adherence: protein A and coagulase only"],"e":"The four abilities and factors: adherence to nasal mucosa (adhesins); invading tissues (cytotoxins, enzymes); evading immune clearance (protein A, capsule); and replicating and disseminating.","w":["Adherence uses adhesins; cytotoxins and enzymes are for invasion.","Invasion uses cytotoxins and enzymes.","Adhesins are for adherence.","Adherence uses adhesins, not protein A."],"k":"Adhesins → adhere; cytotoxins/enzymes → invade; protein A/capsule → evade.","rn":[["","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Protein A; Capsule"]]},{"id":39074,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"The speaker notes group S. aureus virulence factors into three groups. Which is one of them?","o":["Exotoxins that damage host tissues or provoke symptoms","Endotoxins in an outer membrane sensed by TLR4","Flagella that let it swim towards host cells","Spores that survive autoclaving at 121 °C for 20 minutes","Pili used to exchange DNA with viruses"],"e":"The three groups are: surface proteins that promote colonisation of host tissues; surface factors that inhibit phagocytic engulfment (capsule, protein A); and exotoxins that damage host tissues or provoke symptoms (SEA-G, TSST, ET). Gram positive bacteria such as S. aureus do not have endotoxin.","w":["Endotoxin (LPS) belongs to Gram negative bacteria.","Flagella are not listed.","Spores are not listed.","Pili exchanging DNA with viruses are not described."],"k":"S. aureus factors: colonisation proteins, anti-phagocytic factors, exotoxins."},{"id":39075,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"What does protein A of S. aureus bind?","o":["The Fc region of IgG","Host proteins that help it stick to the nasal mucosa","Toll-like receptor 4 on macrophages","Lipopolysaccharide in the outer membrane","Sheep serum in the coagulase test"],"e":"Protein A binds the IgG Fc region and is a surface factor that inhibits phagocytic engulfment (immune evasion). Adhesins help S. aureus stick to host proteins. Worksheet 3 notes that a statement reversing these functions is wrong.","w":["Sticking to host proteins is the role of adhesins.","TLR4 senses LPS from Gram negatives.","S. aureus has no LPS outer membrane.","Clotting of serum is due to coagulase."],"k":"Protein A binds IgG Fc → immune evasion."},{"id":39076,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","a":["protein a"],"s":"Name the S. aureus surface factor that binds the Fc region of IgG and helps it evade immune clearance.","e":"Protein A binds the IgG Fc region and, with the capsule, helps S. aureus evade immune clearance by inhibiting phagocytic engulfment.","k":"Protein A binds IgG Fc.","rn":[["","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Protein A; Capsule"]]},{"id":39077,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"A patient has a necrotising S. aureus infection caused by a strain making Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL). What does PVL do?","o":["It forms pores that lyse polymorphs","It acts as a protease that targets epidermal structural proteins","It is a superantigen causing massive T cell activation","It clots plasma to wall off the infection","It binds the Fc region of IgG"],"e":"Cytotoxins are pore-forming toxins that lyse host cells; Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) lyses polymorphs (immune cells). Exfoliative toxins are proteases targeting epidermal structural proteins; enterotoxins are superantigens causing massive T cell activation.","w":["That describes exfoliative toxins.","That describes enterotoxins (superantigens).","Clotting is the action of coagulase.","Binding IgG Fc is the action of protein A."],"k":"PVL: pore-forming cytotoxin lysing polymorphs.","rn":[["","What do S. aureus cytotoxins do?","What do S. aureus cytotoxins do?","Pore forming, lyse host cells"],["","What does Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) lyse?","What does Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) lyse?","Polymorphs (immune cells)"]]},{"id":39078,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which pairing of S. aureus toxin group and action is correct?","o":["Exfoliative toxins: proteases targeting epidermal structural proteins","Enterotoxins: pore-forming toxins that lyse polymorphs","Cytotoxins: superantigens causing vomiting when ingested","Exfoliative toxins: bind the Fc region of IgG on the surface to block phagocytic engulfment","Enterotoxins: enzymes that clot sheep serum"],"e":"Cytotoxins are pore-forming toxins that lyse host cells (PVL lyses polymorphs). Exfoliative toxins are proteases that target epidermal structural proteins. Enterotoxins are superantigens that stimulate massive T cell activation and immune evasion; ingestion causes vomiting.","w":["Pore formation is the action of cytotoxins.","Superantigen activity is the action of enterotoxins.","IgG Fc binding is protein A.","Clotting serum is coagulase."],"k":"Cytotoxin = pores; exfoliative = epidermal proteases; enterotoxin = superantigen.","rn":[["","What do exfoliative toxins target?","What do exfoliative toxins target?","Epidermal structural proteins"]]},{"id":39079,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Why are many S. aureus toxins found in only a proportion of strains?","o":["Many are phage encoded","They are made only in the stationary phase of growth","They are destroyed by the coagulase test","They require LPS to be expressed","They are produced only inside macrophages"],"e":"The speaker notes state that many of these toxins are phage encoded, so they are only present in a proportion of strains. This is one reason virulence determinants can distinguish more and less virulent S. aureus strains.","w":["Growth phase affects when proteins are made, not which strains carry them.","The coagulase test does not destroy toxins.","S. aureus has no LPS.","Production inside macrophages is not described."],"k":"Phage-encoded toxins vary between strains.","rn":[["","Why are many S. aureus toxins only in some strains?","Why are many S. aureus toxins only in some strains?","Phage encoded"]]},{"id":39080,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","a":["superantigen","superantigens","enterotoxin","enterotoxins"],"s":"What term describes S. aureus enterotoxins that stimulate massive T cell activation?","e":"S. aureus enterotoxins act as superantigens, stimulating massive T cell activation and immune evasion; ingestion causes vomiting.","k":"Enterotoxins are superantigens.","rn":[["","What do enterotoxins (superantigens) stimulate?","What do enterotoxins (superantigens) stimulate?","Massive T cell activation; Immune evasion"]]},{"id":39081,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"In this virulence-factor diagram, which group of proteins is made during the stationary phase of growth?","o":["Secreted proteins such as enterotoxin B, TSST-1 and α-toxin","Surface proteins such as protein A and binding proteins","The capsule, peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid of the cell envelope","Penicillin-binding protein and β-lactamase","The cell-wall anchoring domain of surface proteins"],"e":"The figure shows surface proteins (protein A, elastin-, collagen- and fibronectin-binding proteins) made in the exponential-growth phase, and secreted proteins (enterotoxin B, TSST-1, α-toxin) made in the stationary phase. Panel B shows the envelope (capsule, peptidoglycan, lipoteichoic acid, penicillin-binding protein, β-lactamase); panel C a surface protein's anchoring domain.","w":["Surface proteins are made in the exponential phase.","These are envelope structures, not growth-phase secreted proteins.","These envelope components are in panel B.","The anchoring domain is part of panel C."],"k":"Exponential phase: surface proteins; stationary phase: secreted toxins.","f":"cp38","rn":[["","Which S. aureus secreted proteins are exotoxins?","Which S. aureus secreted proteins are exotoxins?","Enterotoxin B; TSST-1; Alpha-toxin"]]},{"id":39082,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"A lab compares two staphylococci on blood agar. Isolate X forms large yellow haemolytic colonies; isolate Y forms small white non-haemolytic colonies. Which identification is most likely?","o":["X is S. aureus; Y is S. epidermidis","X is S. epidermidis; Y is S. aureus","Both are S. aureus","Both are S. epidermidis","X is Legionella; Y is Salmonella"],"e":"S. aureus forms fairly large yellow colonies on rich medium and is often haemolytic on blood agar; S. epidermidis forms relatively small white colonies and is non-haemolytic. S. aureus is coagulase positive and S. epidermidis coagulase negative.","w":["This reverses the identifications.","Y's features fit S. epidermidis.","X's features fit S. aureus.","These are staphylococcal features."],"k":"S. aureus: large, yellow, haemolytic; S. epidermidis: small, white, non-haemolytic.","f":"cp40","rn":[["","S. aureus vs S. epidermidis: colony","S. aureus vs S. epidermidis: colony","S. aureus: fairly large, yellow; S. epidermidis: relatively small, white"],["","S. aureus vs S. epidermidis: haemolysis on blood agar","S. aureus vs S. epidermidis: haemolysis on blood agar","S. aureus: often haemolytic; S. epidermidis: non haemolytic"]]},{"id":39083,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"What are virulence determinants useful for, according to the lecture?","o":["Distinguishing S. aureus from less virulent staphylococci and comparing strains","Calculating the incubation period of S. aureus infections after colonisation of the nose","Deciding the autoclave temperature for equipment","Choosing the portal of exit for a pathogen","Measuring the size of aerosols"],"e":"Virulence determinants let you distinguish S. aureus from other less virulent staphylococci, such as S. epidermidis, and distinguish more and less virulent S. aureus strains.","w":["Incubation periods are timeline measures.","Autoclave settings are fixed sterilisation conditions.","Portals of exit are a chain-of-infection concept.","Aerosol size is a transmission concept."],"k":"Virulence determinants separate species and strains by virulence.","rn":[["","What do S. aureus virulence determinants distinguish?","What do S. aureus virulence determinants distinguish?","S. aureus vs S. epidermidis; More vs less virulent strains"]]},{"id":39084,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Bacteria are added to sheep serum and incubated. Tube 1 clots; tube 2 stays liquid. What do the results indicate?","o":["Tube 1 is S. aureus (coagulase positive); tube 2 is S. epidermidis (coagulase negative)","Tube 1 is S. epidermidis (coagulase positive); tube 2 is S. aureus (coagulase negative)","Both tubes contain coagulase-negative organisms","Both tubes contain S. aureus","The test measures haemolysis, not clotting"],"e":"The coagulase test: add bacteria to sheep serum, incubate and look for a clot. S. aureus clots the serum (coagulase positive); S. epidermidis leaves it liquid (coagulase negative). Coagulase stimulates clotting and has a role in immune evasion.","w":["This reverses the results.","Tube 1 clotted, so it is coagulase positive.","Tube 2 did not clot, so it is not S. aureus.","The test looks for a clot."],"k":"Coagulase test: clot = S. aureus; no clot = S. epidermidis.","f":"cp41","rn":[["","Image card: Coagulase test (bacteria added to sheep serum): which tube is S. aureus? Why?","Coagulase test (bacteria added to sheep serum): which tube is S. aureus? Why?","Left: clotted (coagulase positive); Right: S. epidermidis (negative)"]]},{"id":39085,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","a":["coagulase"],"s":"Name the S. aureus enzyme that clots serum and has a role in immune evasion.","e":"Coagulase stimulates clotting and has a role in immune evasion. S. aureus is coagulase positive; S. epidermidis is coagulase negative.","k":"Coagulase: clotting enzyme of S. aureus.","rn":[["","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","Stimulates clotting; Immune evasion"]]},{"id":39086,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which statement about coagulase is correct?","o":["It stimulates clotting and helps immune evasion; S. aureus is positive","It is found only in S. epidermidis, which is why that organism clots sheep serum","It breaks down the bacterial cell wall","It is the same as lipopolysaccharide","It makes colonies appear white on agar"],"e":"Coagulase stimulates clotting and has a role in immune evasion. S. aureus is coagulase positive, and the coagulase test (clot in sheep serum) distinguishes it from coagulase-negative S. epidermidis.","w":["S. epidermidis is coagulase negative.","Breaking the cell wall describes lysozyme, not coagulase.","LPS is a Gram negative endotoxin.","S. aureus colonies are yellow/golden."],"k":"Coagulase: clotting, immune evasion; S. aureus positive.","rn":[["","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","Stimulates clotting; Immune evasion"]]},{"id":39087,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which statement about why S. aureus is described as an aggressive human pathogen is correct?","o":["It has a whole range of virulence factors, some on its surface and some excreted","It has a single toxin that explains all of the different diseases it causes in humans","It produces endotoxin like Gram negative bacteria","It can only survive in soil","It never colonises the nose"],"e":"S. aureus is a perfectly adapted, aggressive human pathogen because it has a whole range of distinct virulence factors, some expressed at its surface and some excreted into the environment. It is Gram positive and has no endotoxin.","w":["It has many factors, not one.","Gram positive bacteria have no endotoxin.","It is a nasal commensal of humans.","It colonises the anterior nares."],"k":"S. aureus: many surface and secreted virulence factors.","rn":[["","Why is S. aureus a well adapted pathogen?","Why is S. aureus a well adapted pathogen?","Whole range of virulence factors"]]},{"id":39088,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which surface factors help S. aureus inhibit phagocytic engulfment?","o":["Capsule and protein A","Adhesins and coagulase","Lipopolysaccharide and TLR4","Exfoliative toxins and enterotoxins","Penicillin-binding protein and β-lactamase"],"e":"The speaker notes list surface factors that inhibit phagocytic engulfment as the capsule and protein A. Adhesins help adherence; coagulase clots; exotoxins damage tissues or provoke symptoms.","w":["Adhesins help adherence, and coagulase clots.","LPS and TLR4 relate to Gram negatives.","These are exotoxins.","These are envelope components in the figure, not the anti-phagocytic factors listed."],"k":"Capsule + protein A inhibit phagocytosis.","rn":[["","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Which S. aureus factors help it evade immune clearance?","Protein A; Capsule"],["","What are the 3 groups of S. aureus virulence factors?","What are the 3 groups of S. aureus virulence factors?","Surface proteins promoting colonisation; Surface factors inhibiting phagocytosis; Exotoxins"]]},{"id":39089,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which S. aureus factors help it invade tissues?","o":["Cytotoxins and enzymes","Protein A and the capsule","Adhesins alone","Lipoteichoic acid alone","Coagulase alone"],"e":"The four abilities table: adherence to nasal mucosa (adhesins); invade tissues (cytotoxins, enzymes); evade immune clearance (protein A, capsule); replicate and disseminate.","w":["Protein A and capsule help evade immune clearance.","Adhesins help adherence.","Lipoteichoic acid is a cell wall component that stimulates immunity.","Coagulase is a clotting/immune-evasion factor."],"k":"Tissue invasion: cytotoxins and enzymes.","rn":[["","Which S. aureus factors help it invade tissues?","Which S. aureus factors help it invade tissues?","Cytotoxins; Enzymes"]]},{"id":39090,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which statement about S. aureus surface proteins in the figure is correct?","o":["They include protein A and elastin-, collagen- and fibronectin-binding proteins","They include enterotoxin B and TSST-1","They are made only in the stationary phase","They are all secreted into the environment during the stationary phase of growth","They include lipopolysaccharide"],"e":"The virulence-factor figure lists surface proteins made in the exponential-growth phase: protein A and elastin-, collagen- and fibronectin-binding proteins. Secreted proteins made in the stationary phase include enterotoxin B, TSST-1 and α-toxin. S. aureus has no LPS.","w":["Enterotoxin B and TSST-1 are secreted proteins.","Surface proteins are made in the exponential phase.","Surface proteins stay at the surface.","LPS is a Gram negative molecule."],"k":"S. aureus surface proteins: protein A and matrix-binding proteins.","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of a surface protein such as clumping factor?","What are the 3 parts of a surface protein such as clumping factor?","Cell-wall-anchoring domain; Repeats; Ligand-binding domain"],["","S. aureus wall: 2 penicillin-related molecules?","S. aureus wall: 2 penicillin-related molecules?","Penicillin-binding protein; Beta-lactamase"]]},{"id":39091,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"A child has red sores around the nose and mouth that burst and leave crusty, golden-brown patches. Which S. aureus skin infection is this?","o":["Impetigo","Furunculosis, an infection of a single hair follicle","An abscess, larger, raised and full of pus","A whitlow, an infected fingertip in a food handler","Osteomyelitis seeded from the bloodstream"],"e":"Impetigo starts as red sores or blisters that quickly burst and leave crusty, golden-brown patches, usually on exposed skin such as the nose, mouth and arms. Furunculosis is a boil or hair follicle infection; an abscess is pus-filled, larger and raised; a whitlow is an infected fingertip; bullous impetigo is the blistering form.","w":["Furunculosis is a boil or hair follicle infection.","An abscess is a larger, raised, pus-filled lesion.","A whitlow affects the fingertip.","Osteomyelitis is a metastatic focus of bacteraemia, not a skin rash."],"k":"Impetigo: sores/blisters → golden-brown crusts on exposed skin.","rn":[["","Image card: S. aureus skin infection with crusty golden-brown patches: what is this?","S. aureus skin infection with crusty golden-brown patches: what is this?","Impetigo"],["","How does impetigo progress?","How does impetigo progress?","Sores burst; Crusty golden-brown patches"],["","How does impetigo start?","How does impetigo start?","Red sores or blisters"]]},{"id":39092,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"Which S. aureus skin infection is described as a boil, or infection of the hair follicle?","o":["Furunculosis","Impetigo","Bullous impetigo","Abscess","Whitlow"],"e":"Furunculosis is a boil, or infection of the hair follicle. Impetigo leaves golden-brown crusts; bullous impetigo is the blistering form; an abscess is pus-filled, larger and raised; a whitlow (felon) is an infected fingertip.","w":["Impetigo forms golden-brown crusts.","Bullous impetigo is blistering.","An abscess is larger and pus-filled.","A whitlow is a fingertip infection."],"k":"Furunculosis = boil / hair follicle infection.","rn":[["","What is furunculosis?","What is furunculosis?","A boil; Hair follicle infection"],["","Image card: S. aureus skin infection: what is this?","S. aureus skin infection: what is this?","Furunculosis (boils, hair follicle infection)"]]},{"id":39093,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"This collage shows several S. aureus skin infections. Which lesion in the bottom row is a blistering form?","o":["Bullous impetigo","Furunculosis","Impetigo on the face","Whitlow","Septic arthritis"],"e":"The skin infections slide shows furunculosis and impetigo (arm and face) on the top row, and a staph abscess and bullous impetigo on the bottom row. Bullous impetigo is the blistering form of impetigo.","w":["Furunculosis is a boil on the top row.","Facial impetigo is on the top row.","The whitlow is on a separate slide.","Septic arthritis is a metastatic infection, not on this skin slide."],"k":"Bullous impetigo = blistering impetigo.","f":"cp42","rn":[["","Image card: Blistering S. aureus skin infection: what is this?","Blistering S. aureus skin infection: what is this?","Bullous impetigo"]]},{"id":39094,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"A patient with a central venous line develops S. aureus bacteraemia and then a new heart murmur. Which statement best explains the murmur?","o":["Bacteraemia can seed metastatic foci such as endocarditis","S. aureus never enters the bloodstream","The murmur is caused by enterotoxin-induced vomiting","Coagulase-negative S. aureus forms vegetations in the bladder","The line infection is a form of impetigo"],"e":"S. aureus ranges from normal commensal to skin and soft tissue infections, surgical site infections and vascular line-related infections. It is the commonest cause of bacteraemia, which can seed secondary \"metastatic\" foci: endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis and almost anywhere else.","w":["S. aureus is the commonest cause of bacteraemia.","Enterotoxins cause food poisoning, not murmurs.","S. aureus is coagulase positive.","Impetigo is a superficial skin infection."],"k":"S. aureus bacteraemia seeds endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.","rn":[["","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","Endocarditis; Osteomyelitis; Septic arthritis"]]},{"id":39095,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a metastatic focus of S. aureus bacteraemia?","o":["Legionnaires' disease","Endocarditis","Osteomyelitis","Septic arthritis","Infection almost anywhere else"],"e":"S. aureus bacteraemia can seed secondary \"metastatic\" foci of infection: endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis and almost anywhere else. Legionnaires' disease is caused by Legionella pneumophila from a water reservoir.","w":["Endocarditis is listed.","Osteomyelitis is listed.","Septic arthritis is listed.","Almost anywhere else is listed."],"k":"Metastatic foci: endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, anywhere.","rn":[["","Which disease comes from the water reservoir organism?","Which disease comes from the water reservoir organism?","Legionnaires' disease"]]},{"id":39096,"t":"S. aureus Disease","a":["bacteraemia","bacteremia"],"s":"S. aureus is the commonest cause of which bloodstream condition, meaning bacteria in the blood?","e":"S. aureus is the commonest cause of bacteraemia (bacteria in the blood), which can seed metastatic foci such as endocarditis, osteomyelitis and septic arthritis.","k":"S. aureus: commonest cause of bacteraemia.","rn":[["","S. aureus is the commonest cause of what?","S. aureus is the commonest cause of what?","Bacteraemia"],["","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","Endocarditis; Osteomyelitis; Septic arthritis"]]},{"id":39097,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"Several guests vomit repeatedly within hours of eating cream cakes made by a caterer with an infected fingertip, with little diarrhoea, and recover quickly. Which explanation fits best?","o":["S. aureus enterotoxins contaminated the food during preparation","Salmonella from poultry multiplied in the guests' guts over several days","Rotavirus spread from a human gut reservoir","Legionella from the water supply","Clostridium tetani from soil entered through the mouth"],"e":"S. aureus food poisoning is caused by S. aureus enterotoxins. After ingestion there is a rapid, brief illness with vomiting +++ and minimal diarrhoea. Food becomes contaminated during manufacture or preparation; the slide pictures a whitlow, an infected fingertip, on a food handler.","w":["Salmonella is a poultry-associated gastroenteritis, not this rapid vomiting pattern.","Rotavirus is a human gut reservoir example.","Legionella causes Legionnaires' disease.","C. tetani causes tetanus via wounds."],"k":"Staph food poisoning: preformed enterotoxin → fast vomiting, minimal diarrhoea.","rn":[["","At what 2 stages is food contaminated by S. aureus?","At what 2 stages is food contaminated by S. aureus?","Manufacture; Preparation"],["","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","S. aureus enterotoxins"],["","What are the 3 features of S. aureus food poisoning?","What are the 3 features of S. aureus food poisoning?","Rapid brief illness; Vomiting +++; Minimal diarrhoea"]]},{"id":39098,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"This image is shown in the context of S. aureus food poisoning. What is the lesion, and why is it relevant?","o":["A whitlow (felon) on a food handler's finger that can contaminate food","Bullous impetigo on the face of a child","A tetanus wound contaminated with soil","An HSV cold sore reactivating","A shingles rash along a dermatome"],"e":"The slide pictures a whitlow (or felon), a swollen infected fingertip: an S. aureus skin infection on a food handler's hand. Food becomes contaminated with S. aureus during manufacture or preparation, and its enterotoxins cause food poisoning.","w":["Bullous impetigo is on the skin infections slide.","Tetanus is not the subject of this image.","Cold sores are HSV reactivation, unrelated here.","Shingles is VZV reactivation, unrelated here."],"k":"Whitlow on a food handler → S. aureus food contamination.","f":"cp44","rn":[["","Image card: Infected fingertip on a food handler: what is this? Which organism?","Infected fingertip on a food handler: what is this? Which organism?","Whitlow (or felon); S. aureus"]]},{"id":39099,"t":"S. aureus Disease","a":["enterotoxins","enterotoxin","staphylococcal enterotoxins"],"s":"Which S. aureus products cause staphylococcal food poisoning?","e":"Staphylococcal food poisoning is caused by S. aureus enterotoxins. Ingestion causes a rapid, brief illness with marked vomiting and minimal diarrhoea.","k":"Staph food poisoning is caused by enterotoxins.","rn":[["","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","S. aureus enterotoxins"]]},{"id":39100,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","s":"A patient with Gram negative bacteraemia becomes rapidly and severely unwell. According to the lecture, what is the main reason?","o":["Endotoxin (LPS) in the blood causes systemic immune activation via TLR4","Lipoteichoic acid activates TLR4 in the gut","Coagulase clots the patient's blood","Protein A binds the Fc region of IgG","Enterotoxins cause massive vomiting"],"e":"Gram negative bacteria in the blood release endotoxin (LPS) into the blood, causing systemic activation of immune responses. LPS interacts with Toll-like receptors, especially TLR4, on monocyte/macrophage lineage cells and endothelial cells, activating inflammatory and clotting pathways and changing endothelial integrity. This is the main reason people get very sick with Gram negative infections.","w":["Lipoteichoic acid is a Gram positive component acting through other TLRs.","Coagulase is an S. aureus enzyme.","Protein A is an S. aureus factor.","Enterotoxins cause food poisoning."],"k":"Gram negative sepsis: LPS → TLR4 → systemic immune activation.","rn":[["","Why do people get very sick with Gram negative infections?","Why do people get very sick with Gram negative infections?","Endotoxin in blood; Systemic immune activation"]]},{"id":39101,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","s":"Which effects of LPS are listed in the lecture?","o":["Activation of inflammatory and clotting pathways and changes in endothelial integrity","Formation of golden colonies on blood agar","Lysis of polymorphs by pore formation","Clotting of sheep serum in a test tube","Production of spores in the soil"],"e":"LPS activates inflammatory pathways, coagulation and clotting pathways, and changes in endothelial integrity. It acts through TLRs, especially TLR4, on monocyte/macrophage lineage cells and endothelial cells.","w":["Golden colonies are an S. aureus lab feature.","Pore formation is PVL's action.","Clotting serum is coagulase.","Spores are not discussed."],"k":"LPS: inflammation, coagulation, endothelial changes.","rn":[["","What 3 things does LPS recognition activate?","What 3 things does LPS recognition activate?","Inflammatory pathways; Coagulation and clotting pathways; Changes in endothelial integrity"]]},{"id":39102,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","s":"Why might the innate immune system be so sensitive to LPS, according to the lecture?","o":["Gram negatives predominate in bites and contaminated wounds, so early recognition helps","LPS is found on every human cell","LPS is made by S. aureus in the nose","LPS is a harmless food antigen","LPS is produced only by viruses"],"e":"The innate immune system is very sensitive to LPS, probably for evolutionary reasons: Gram negatives predominate in bites, contaminated wounds and so on. Sensitivity allows very early recognition of infection.","w":["LPS is not on human cells.","S. aureus is Gram positive and has no LPS.","LPS is a bacterial endotoxin, not a food antigen.","LPS is a Gram negative bacterial molecule."],"k":"LPS sensitivity allows very early recognition of Gram negatives.","rn":[["","Why is innate immunity so sensitive to LPS?","Why is innate immunity so sensitive to LPS?","Gram negatives in bites/wounds; Very early recognition"]]},{"id":39103,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts Gram negative and Gram positive immune triggers?","o":["Gram negatives trigger via LPS and TLR4; Gram positives via lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan through other TLRs","Gram positives trigger via LPS and TLR4; Gram negatives have no immune trigger","Both rely only on LPS acting through TLR4","Gram positives have endotoxin; Gram negatives do not","Neither interacts with Toll-like receptors"],"e":"Gram negative bacteria have LPS (endotoxin), sensed especially by TLR4. Gram positive bacteria do not have endotoxin; their cell wall components lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate immune responses similar to LPS, but through different Toll-like receptors.","w":["LPS belongs to Gram negatives.","Gram positives use LTA and peptidoglycan.","Gram positives lack endotoxin.","Both interact with TLRs."],"k":"Gram −: LPS/TLR4; Gram +: LTA, peptidoglycan/other TLRs.","rn":[["","LPS vs lipoteichoic acid: receptors","LPS vs lipoteichoic acid: receptors","LPS: TLR4 especially; Lipoteichoic acid: different TLRs"],["","Which 2 Gram positive cell wall components trigger immunity?","Which 2 Gram positive cell wall components trigger immunity?","Lipoteichoic acid; Peptidoglycans"],["","What do lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate?","What do lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate?","Immune responses similar to LPS"]]},{"id":39104,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","a":["tlr4","toll-like receptor 4","toll like receptor 4"],"s":"Name the Toll-like receptor especially associated with recognising LPS.","e":"LPS interacts with Toll-like receptors, especially TLR4, on monocyte/macrophage lineage cells and endothelial cells.","k":"LPS is sensed especially by TLR4.","rn":[["","LPS interacts with which receptor especially?","LPS interacts with which receptor especially?","TLR4 (Toll-like receptor 4)"]]},{"id":39105,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","a":["lipoteichoic acid","lta"],"s":"Name the Gram positive cell wall component, besides peptidoglycan, that stimulates immune responses similar to LPS.","e":"Gram positive bacteria lack endotoxin, but lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans in their cell wall stimulate immune responses similar to LPS through different Toll-like receptors.","k":"Lipoteichoic acid: Gram positive immune trigger.","rn":[["","Which 2 Gram positive cell wall components trigger immunity?","Which 2 Gram positive cell wall components trigger immunity?","Lipoteichoic acid; Peptidoglycans"],["","What do lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate?","What do lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate?","Immune responses similar to LPS"]]},{"id":39106,"t":"MRSA Case Study","s":"Ricardo, a well patient on a ward, has MRSA on his nasal swab but no symptoms. Which interpretation is correct?","o":["He is colonised, not infected, but should still have eradication treatment","He has an opportunistic infection, so he must be immunocompromised","No infection control is needed because he is well","MRSA is a commensal, so it is not pathogenic","He must carry a low-virulence strain lacking adhesins and coagulase"],"e":"Ricardo is asymptomatic with a working immune system, so he is colonised, not infected. Eradication with shampoos and nasal creams protects others and stops him becoming ill from overgrowth if his immunity drops or antibiotics remove competing commensals. Infection control is still needed, MRSA is pathogenic, and being well does not mean low virulence.","w":["He is asymptomatic with working immunity, so he is colonised, not infected.","Infection control is needed because other patients, like Gerald, are vulnerable.","An organism is either pathogenic or not; MRSA is.","Host factors shape virulence; the strain could badly harm others."],"k":"Well MRSA carrier = colonised; still eradicate and control spread.","rn":[["","Should a colonised MRSA carrier be treated?","Should a colonised MRSA carrier be treated?","Yes: eradication"],["","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","Colonised, not infected"]]},{"id":39107,"t":"MRSA Case Study","s":"What infection-control action is most appropriate for Ricardo, the MRSA-colonised patient opposite an unwell patient?","o":["Move him to a side room and deep clean his previous bay","Leave him in the bay, because colonised patients cannot spread MRSA","Tell him to avoid other patients, which prevents all spread","Stop all hand hygiene on the ward to avoid skin damage","Move the unwell patient into Ricardo's bed"],"e":"Moving Ricardo to a side room and deep cleaning his previous bay breaks the chain; cleaning fomites prevents indirect spread. In practice side rooms are scarce, so patients are often barrier nursed (aprons and gloves around the bed). Avoiding other patients does not stop indirect transmission via items others handle.","w":["Colonised patients can spread MRSA to vulnerable patients.","Indirect transmission via fomites can still occur.","Hand hygiene is a key MRSA control measure.","This would expose the vulnerable patient to the contaminated area."],"k":"MRSA carrier: side room/barrier nursing and deep clean.","rn":[["","Why deep clean the previous bay of an MRSA carrier?","Why deep clean the previous bay of an MRSA carrier?","Remove fomites (indirect spread)"]]},{"id":39108,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"Which statement about S. aureus from the case-study options is correct?","o":["It is coagulase positive, which helps clotting and immune evasion","It is not a commensal of humans","It is coagulase negative like S. epidermidis","Its adhesins bind IgG Fc and protein A helps adhesion","It cannot spread indirectly via objects"],"e":"Worksheet 3: S. aureus is a commensal (30% of the population carry it on skin, 10% of which is MRSA); it is coagulase positive (clotting, immune evasion); protein A binds the IgG Fc region and adhesins help it stick to host proteins; and it can spread indirectly via items others handle.","w":["It is a commensal carried by many people.","S. aureus is coagulase positive.","The functions are reversed: protein A binds IgG Fc; adhesins help adhesion.","Indirect transmission via fomites occurs."],"k":"S. aureus: commensal, coagulase positive.","rn":[["","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","What are the 2 roles of coagulase?","Stimulates clotting; Immune evasion"]]},{"id":39109,"t":"MRSA Case Study","s":"According to worksheet 3, what proportion of the population carry S. aureus on their skin, and how much of this is MRSA?","o":["30% carry it; 10% of this is MRSA","60% carry it; half of this is MRSA","100% carry it; none is MRSA","1% carry it; all of it is MRSA","10% carry it; 30% of this is MRSA"],"e":"Worksheet 3 states that 30% of the population have S. aureus on their skin, and 10% of this is MRSA. The slide gives 20–60% nasal carriers, and the notes give 20% persistent, 60% intermittent and 20% almost never.","w":["These figures are not given.","Not everyone carries it.","Carriage is far more common than 1%.","The two numbers are swapped."],"k":"Worksheet 3: 30% carry S. aureus on skin; 10% of that is MRSA.","rn":[["","What % of people carry S. aureus on skin?","What % of people carry S. aureus on skin?","30%"],["","What % of S. aureus carriage is MRSA?","What % of S. aureus carriage is MRSA?","10%"]]},{"id":39110,"t":"MRSA Case Study","a":["colonisation","colonised","colonization","colonized"],"s":"What term describes carrying an organism without being infected by it, as in a well MRSA carrier?","e":"Colonisation means carrying an organism without being infected by it. The worksheet stresses: colonised is not the same as infected, but a well MRSA carrier still needs eradication and isolation or barrier nursing.","k":"Colonised ≠ infected.","rn":[["","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","Colonised, not infected"],["","Should a colonised MRSA carrier be treated?","Should a colonised MRSA carrier be treated?","Yes: eradication"]]},{"id":39111,"t":"MRSA Case Study","s":"Gerald, aged 68, is admitted confused, with headache and green sputum, and is MRSA-negative. Why does Ricardo's MRSA colonisation still matter for Gerald?","o":["Gerald is already fighting an infection and is vulnerable","Gerald is colonised with MRSA too, so it makes no difference","MRSA cannot infect people over 65","Ricardo's MRSA is a commensal and harmless to all","Gerald's negative swab means he is immune to MRSA"],"e":"The case answers state that infection control is needed because Gerald is already fighting an infection, so he is vulnerable; Ricardo should be in a side room or at least barrier nursed. MRSA is pathogenic, and a strain harmless to a well carrier could badly harm others.","w":["Gerald's swab was negative.","Age does not prevent MRSA infection; extremes of age increase risk.","MRSA is pathogenic.","A negative swab does not mean immunity."],"k":"Vulnerable patients need protection from colonised carriers."},{"id":39112,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"An organism is described as having high pathogenicity but varying virulence between strains. Which S. aureus feature best explains strain-to-strain variation?","o":["Many toxins are phage encoded and present in only some strains","All S. aureus strains have identical toxins","S. aureus has no surface proteins","S. aureus is always coagulase negative","S. aureus produces LPS in some strains only"],"e":"Many S. aureus toxins are phage encoded, so they are only present in a proportion of strains. Virulence determinants can therefore distinguish more and less virulent S. aureus strains as well as separate S. aureus from less virulent staphylococci.","w":["Toxin content varies between strains.","S. aureus has many surface proteins.","S. aureus is coagulase positive.","S. aureus is Gram positive and lacks LPS."],"k":"Phage-encoded toxins explain strain variation in virulence.","rn":[["","Why are many S. aureus toxins only in some strains?","Why are many S. aureus toxins only in some strains?","Phage encoded"]]},{"id":39113,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"Which chain-of-infection link is broken by giving a hospital worker an immunisation?","o":["The reservoir (occupational health)","The portal of exit","The mode of transmission","The portal of entry","The causative agent by culture"],"e":"Occupational health measures, immunisation and regular check-ups of staff and restriction from patient contact when they have a communicable disease, are listed under controlling the reservoir. Immunising patients is listed under protecting the susceptible host.","w":["Masks, gloves, asepsis and waste handling target the exit.","Contact, droplet and airborne precautions target the mode.","Skin integrity, sharps and catheters target the entry.","Culturing identifies the agent."],"k":"Staff immunisation: reservoir control (occupational health).","rn":[["","3 occupational health reservoir controls?","3 occupational health reservoir controls?","Immunisation; Regular check ups; Infected staff: no patient contact"]]},{"id":39114,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A patient's latent herpes simplex virus reactivates during a period of immunosuppression. Which combination of terms applies?","o":["Endogenous infection; latent infection with reactivation","Exogenous infection; acute primary infection","Zoonotic transmission; chronic bacterial infection","Iatrogenic transmission; subclinical bacterial infection","Vertical transmission; apparent primary infection"],"e":"Reactivation of a virus latent in host cells (e.g. recurrent HSV) is an endogenous infection. Latent infection is a persistent, symptom-free infection after primary infection, with symptoms appearing later. Immunosurveillance controls latent viruses, and immunosuppression or ageing can lead to reactivation.","w":["The virus was already inside the host, so it is not exogenous or a new primary infection.","HSV is a human virus, not a zoonosis or a bacterium.","Reactivation is not caused by a procedure.","This is not a new infection from a parent."],"k":"HSV reactivation = endogenous latent infection.","rn":[["","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","Lies latent in cells; Reactivates later"],["","What are 2 good examples of latent infection?","What are 2 good examples of latent infection?","HIV; HSV-1 or HSV-2 reactivation"]]},{"id":39115,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"Which pairing of term and definition is NOT correct?","o":["Opportunistic pathogen: almost always causes disease","Commensal: benefits from the host without harm","Pathogenicity: capacity to cause disease","Virulence: degree of pathogenicity","Professional pathogen: almost always causes disease"],"e":"An opportunistic pathogen only causes disease in immunocompromised patients; it is the professional pathogen that almost always causes disease. The other definitions are correct.","w":["This is the correct definition of a commensal.","This is the correct definition of pathogenicity.","This is the correct definition of virulence.","This is the correct definition of a professional pathogen."],"k":"Opportunistic pathogens cause disease only in the immunocompromised.","rn":[["","What is a professional pathogen?","What is a professional pathogen?","Almost always causes disease"],["","What is an opportunistic pathogen?","What is an opportunistic pathogen?","Only causes disease in immunocompromised"]]},{"id":39116,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"On a ward, a patient with chickenpox is admitted. Varicella zoster spreads by droplet, fomites, skin contact and airborne routes. Which set of precautions covers the widest route?","o":["Airborne precautions: negative pressure and N95/P2 masks","Droplet precautions only: single room and surgical masks","Contact precautions only: hand hygiene and PPE","No precautions, because chickenpox is a childhood disease","Food hygiene and cold storage"],"e":"Varicella zoster is linked to respiratory droplet, fomites, skin-to-skin contact and airborne spread. Airborne aerosols travel furthest (>2 m) and are hardest to control, needing negative pressure rooms and N95/P2 masks, which also cover the shorter-range routes when combined with contact measures.","w":["Droplet precautions do not contain aerosols.","Contact precautions alone do not stop airborne spread.","Precautions are needed whatever the age.","Food hygiene is for faeco-oral organisms."],"k":"Airborne-spread organisms need negative pressure + N95/P2.","rn":[["","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","Negative pressure; N95/P2 masks; Limit movement outside room"]]},{"id":39117,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"An immunocompromised patient's long-term catheter becomes infected with Staphylococcus epidermidis. Which two chain-of-infection links are most relevant?","o":["Portal of entry (catheter) and susceptible host (immunocompromised)","Reservoir (soil) and mode (vector)","Portal of exit (tears) and mode (transplacental)","Causative agent (malaria) and reservoir (water)","Mode (airborne) and portal of exit (semen)"],"e":"Catheters and invasive devices are portals of entry, and careful handling of catheters is a portal-of-entry measure. Immunocompromised patients are susceptible hosts in whom low-virulence organisms such as S. epidermidis may become pathogens.","w":["Soil and vectors are not involved here.","Tears and transplacental spread are not involved.","Malaria and water reservoirs are not involved.","Airborne spread and semen are not involved."],"k":"Devices = portal of entry; immunosuppression = susceptible host.","rn":[["","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Chain of infection: last 3 links?","Mode of transmission; Portal of entry; Susceptible host"]]},{"id":39118,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"Which statement summarises the routes of transmission given in the lecture summary?","o":["Respiratory, genitourinary and GI tracts, skin and mucous membranes, and placenta","Only the respiratory tract","Only blood transfusions and sharps","Only animal bites and insect vectors","Only the gastrointestinal tract"],"e":"The summary lists routes of transmission as the respiratory, genitourinary and gastrointestinal tracts, skin and mucous membranes, and the placenta.","w":["Several routes are listed, not just respiratory.","Blood and sharps are part of inoculation, but not the whole list.","Vectors are one route among many.","The GI tract is one route among several."],"k":"Transmission routes: respiratory, GU, GI, skin/mucosa, placenta.","rn":[["","Which body routes can infection be transmitted through?","Which body routes can infection be transmitted through?","Respiratory, genitourinary, gastrointestinal tracts; Skin and mucous membranes; Placenta"]]},{"id":39119,"t":"Transmission Routes","a":["nosocomial","nosocomial infection","hospital acquired","hospital-acquired"],"s":"What term describes an infection acquired in hospital or a health care setting?","e":"Nosocomial infection is acquired in hospital or health care. Patients' weakened defences, invasive devices, immunosuppressive drugs and lapses in infection control set the stage for nosocomial infections.","k":"Nosocomial = hospital-acquired.","rn":[["","What is nosocomial infection?","What is nosocomial infection?","Acquired in hospital; Or health care"]]},{"id":39120,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"Which combination best explains why S. aureus can cause such a wide spectrum of disease, from colonisation to bacteraemia?","o":["Many virulence factors, plus host factors such as site and immune state","A single exotoxin that acts identically in every host","It is a professional pathogen that always causes severe disease","Endotoxin acting via TLR4 in every infection","Its inability to colonise the nose"],"e":"S. aureus has a whole range of virulence factors (adhesins, cytotoxins, enzymes, protein A, capsule, coagulase, exotoxins), but pathogenicity also depends on site and the patient's immune state. That is why it can be a harmless nasal commensal in one person and cause skin infections, bacteraemia and metastatic foci in another.","w":["It has many factors, not one, and host factors vary.","It is often a harmless commensal.","S. aureus is Gram positive and has no endotoxin.","It colonises the anterior nares."],"k":"S. aureus disease spectrum = many virulence factors + host factors.","rn":[["","What 3 things does pathogenicity depend on?","What 3 things does pathogenicity depend on?","Organism's virulence; Where it is; Immune state of patient"]]},{"id":39121,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"Twelve guests at a wedding become ill 2–4 hours after eating a cream dessert. All have intense, repeated vomiting with very little diarrhoea, and all recover within a day. The caterer who prepared the dessert has a swollen, infected fingertip.<br><br>Which organism and mechanism best explain the outbreak?","o":["S. aureus enterotoxins contaminating the food during preparation","Campylobacter spreading by the faeco-oral route and invading the gut wall over several days","Salmonella from poultry multiplying in the gut and causing prolonged diarrhoea","Rotavirus shed from the human gut and passed by droplet spread at the reception","Clostridium tetani from soil contaminating the cream and producing toxin"],"e":"Step 1: S. aureus food poisoning is caused by enterotoxins; after ingestion there is a rapid, brief illness with vomiting +++ and minimal diarrhoea. Step 2: food becomes contaminated during manufacture or preparation; the slide pictures a whitlow (infected fingertip), an S. aureus skin infection on a food handler's hand.","w":["Campylobacter is faeco-oral and causes diarrhoea, not this rapid vomiting picture.","Salmonella gastroenteritis is linked to poultry, not a rapid vomiting illness from a handler.","Rotavirus is a gut reservoir example, not spread from a whitlow.","C. tetani has a soil reservoir and causes tetanus."],"k":"Staph food poisoning: preformed enterotoxin, fast, vomiting +++, food handler.","rn":[["","At what 2 stages is food contaminated by S. aureus?","At what 2 stages is food contaminated by S. aureus?","Manufacture; Preparation"],["","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","What causes S. aureus food poisoning?","S. aureus enterotoxins"]]},{"id":39122,"t":"MRSA Case Study","s":"A 68-year-old man is admitted with pneumonia and is MRSA negative. The patient in the opposite bed has MRSA on a nasal swab but is completely well, with no symptoms and a working immune system.<br><br>What is the best interpretation and management of the opposite patient?","o":["Colonised, not infected; eradicate and isolate or barrier nurse","Infected with an opportunistic pathogen, so he must be immunocompromised","Carrying a commensal, so MRSA is not pathogenic and no action is needed","Carrying a low-virulence strain that lacks adhesins, protein A and coagulase","Infected, so he needs no infection control because he is not coughing"],"e":"Step 1: he is asymptomatic with a working immune system, so he is colonised, not infected. Step 2: MRSA is still pathogenic (an organism is either capable of causing disease or not), and the pneumonia patient is vulnerable, so he should be treated to eradicate it (shampoos, nasal creams) and put in a side room or barrier nursed, with his previous bay deep cleaned.","w":["Being well with a working immune system means colonisation, not opportunistic infection.","MRSA is pathogenic even when it is not harming the carrier.","Being well does not mean low virulence; host factors also shape virulence.","He is colonised, and infection control is still needed to protect others."],"k":"Colonised ≠ infected; still eradicate and isolate.","rn":[["","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","A well patient with MRSA on a nasal swab is what?","Colonised, not infected"]]},{"id":39123,"t":"Timeline of Infection","s":"During an outbreak of a respiratory virus, contact tracing shows that people become infectious to others about 2 days after infection, but do not develop symptoms until about 5 days after infection. Screening based only on symptoms fails to control the spread.<br><br>Which explanation fits these findings?","o":["The latent period is shorter than the incubation period, so people spread infection before they feel ill","The incubation period is shorter than the latent period, so people always feel ill before they become infectious","Incubation and latent periods are always identical","People are only infectious during the symptomatic phase","The virus is latent in nerve cells and reactivates"],"e":"Step 1: incubation period is the time from infection to onset of symptoms (5 days here); latent period is the time from infection to infectiousness (2 days). Step 2: when the latent period is shorter, people are infectious before symptoms, as with SARS-CoV-2, so symptom-based screening misses them. Which is shorter depends on the disease.","w":["Here infectiousness comes before symptoms.","They can differ, depending on the disease.","This is not always the case, e.g. SARS-CoV-2.","Latency (dormant infection) is a different concept from the latent period."],"k":"Latent period < incubation → presymptomatic spread.","rn":[["","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Incubation period vs latent period: which is shorter?","Depends on the disease"],["","What is the incubation period?","What is the incubation period?","Infection to symptoms"]]},{"id":39124,"t":"Pathogenicity and Virulence","s":"A 50-year-old receiving chemotherapy develops a bloodstream infection with Staphylococcus epidermidis from her central line, and later oral Candida albicans. Her healthy husband carries both organisms without any illness.<br><br>Which statement best explains the difference?","o":["Low-virulence organisms become pathogens when the patient is immunocompromised","S. epidermidis is a professional pathogen that always causes disease","Her husband's strains must lack all virulence factors","Pathogenicity depends only on the organism, not on the host","Candida and S. epidermidis stay at the commensal end in every patient, whatever their immune state"],"e":"Step 1: S. epidermidis and C. albicans sit in the middle of the commensal–pathogen spectrum. Step 2: in an immunocompromised patient, low-virulence organisms may become pathogens and move to the pathogen end. Pathogenicity depends on the organism's virulence, where it is in the body and the patient's immune state, and the central line gives S. epidermidis a site.","w":["S. epidermidis is not a professional pathogen.","Host immune state, not absent virulence factors, explains the difference.","Pathogenicity also depends on site and immune state.","They move to the pathogen end when the patient is immunocompromised."],"k":"Opportunistic pathogens cause disease in immunocompromised hosts.","rn":[["","In immunocompromised patients, low virulence organisms become?","In immunocompromised patients, low virulence organisms become?","Pathogens"]]},{"id":39125,"t":"Breaking the Chain of Infection","s":"A ward has a patient with an infection spread by aerosols smaller than 5 µm that can float in the air for hours. A second patient has an infection spread by large droplets over 5 µm that fall within about 2 m.<br><br>Which precautions match each patient?","o":["Aerosol: negative pressure and N95/P2 masks; droplet: single room and surgical masks","Aerosol: surgical masks only; droplet: negative pressure and N95 masks","Both: open bay nursing with hand hygiene only","Aerosol: single room and surgical masks; droplet: no precautions needed, as droplets fall within 2 m","Both: autoclave at 121 °C for 20 minutes"],"e":"Step 1: aerosols (&lt;5 µm) travel far and are harder to contain, needing airborne precautions: negative pressure, N95/P2 masks and limited movement outside the room. Step 2: droplets (&gt;5 µm) fall within about 2 m and are easier to contain: droplet precautions are a single patient room, surgical masks and limited movement.","w":["The precautions are swapped.","Both need more than hand hygiene.","Airborne spread needs negative pressure and respirators; droplets still need precautions.","Autoclaving sterilises equipment; it is not a patient precaution."],"k":"Airborne: negative pressure + N95; droplet: single room + surgical mask.","rn":[["","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","What are the 3 airborne precautions?","Negative pressure; N95/P2 masks; Limit movement outside room"],["","What are the 3 droplet precautions?","What are the 3 droplet precautions?","Single patient room; Surgical masks; Limit movement outside room"]]},{"id":39126,"t":"Chain of Infection","s":"A 74-year-old who had chickenpox as a child develops a painful band of blisters on one side of her chest after starting immunosuppressive treatment. Her doctor explains that the virus had been present in her body for decades.<br><br>Which statement best explains this illness?","o":["Reactivation of an endogenous latent virus when immunosurveillance falls","A new exogenous infection acquired from a contaminated object on the ward","A chronic infection with continuous high-level viral replication since childhood","An acute infection that the immune system has never encountered before","A zoonotic infection caught from an animal reservoir"],"e":"Step 1: most viral diseases come from exogenous viruses, but some result from reactivation of an endogenous virus latent in specific host cells, such as shingles (VZV, varicella zoster virus). Step 2: immunosurveillance controls these latent viruses, and ageing or immunosuppression can lead to reactivation.","w":["The virus was already inside her, so this is endogenous.","Latent virus falls to a low level, then flares; this is not continuous high replication.","She was infected decades earlier.","There is no animal source described."],"k":"Shingles = reactivated endogenous latent VZV (ageing, immunosuppression).","rn":[["","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","What does an endogenous virus do in reactivation?","Lies latent in cells; Reactivates later"]]},{"id":39127,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"A wound swab from a 40-year-old grows Gram positive cocci in clusters. Colony A is large, yellow and haemolytic on blood agar, and clots sheep serum in a tube. Colony B is small, white, non-haemolytic, and the serum stays liquid.<br><br>Which identification is correct?","o":["A is S. aureus (coagulase positive); B is S. epidermidis","A is S. epidermidis (coagulase positive); B is S. aureus","Both are S. aureus, as all staphylococci are coagulase positive","A is a Gram negative rod; B is S. aureus","Both are S. epidermidis, as clusters rule out S. aureus"],"e":"Step 1: S. aureus forms fairly large, yellow (golden) colonies that are often haemolytic; S. epidermidis forms small white non-haemolytic colonies. Step 2: in the coagulase test S. aureus clots the serum (positive) and S. epidermidis does not (negative). Coagulase stimulates clotting and has a role in immune evasion.","w":["S. epidermidis is coagulase negative.","S. epidermidis is coagulase negative.","The cocci are Gram positive.","S. aureus forms Gram positive cocci in clusters."],"k":"S. aureus: golden, haemolytic, coagulase positive.","rn":[["","Image card: Coagulase test (bacteria added to sheep serum): which tube is S. aureus? Why?","Coagulase test (bacteria added to sheep serum): which tube is S. aureus? Why?","Left: clotted (coagulase positive); Right: S. epidermidis (negative)"]]},{"id":39128,"t":"S. aureus Disease","s":"A 65-year-old with an infected vascular line develops S. aureus in his blood. Two weeks later he has a new heart murmur, back pain from bone infection and a hot swollen knee.<br><br>Which process best explains these later problems?","o":["Bacteraemia seeding secondary metastatic foci of infection","Enterotoxin ingestion causing a rapid self-limiting illness of the gut","Exfoliative toxins acting only on the epidermis at the line site","A latent virus reactivating in several organs at once","An inapparent infection that has produced no symptoms"],"e":"Step 1: S. aureus progresses from commensal to skin/soft tissue, surgical site and vascular line-related infections, and is the commonest cause of bacteraemia. Step 2: bacteraemia can seed secondary metastatic foci: endocarditis (murmur), osteomyelitis (bone), septic arthritis (knee) and almost anywhere else.","w":["Enterotoxin ingestion causes food poisoning, not these foci.","Exfoliative toxins target epidermal proteins only.","This is bacterial, not viral reactivation.","He clearly has symptoms."],"k":"S. aureus bacteraemia → endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.","rn":[["","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","S. aureus bacteraemia: 3 metastatic foci?","Endocarditis; Osteomyelitis; Septic arthritis"]]},{"id":39129,"t":"LPS and Immune Recognition","s":"Two patients have bacteraemia. Patient 1 has a Gram negative organism and rapidly develops widespread inflammation, clotting abnormalities and leaky blood vessels. Patient 2 has a Gram positive organism.<br><br>Which statement best explains patient 1's severe illness and the difference from patient 2?","o":["LPS acts via TLR4 to activate systemic inflammation; Gram positives lack endotoxin","Gram positives have more LPS, but it is hidden by the capsule, so it cannot reach TLR4 on macrophages","Lipoteichoic acid activates TLR4 more strongly than LPS does","LPS acts only on red blood cells, while peptidoglycan acts on platelets","Gram negative bacteria cannot be recognised by the innate immune system"],"e":"Step 1: LPS (endotoxin) in the outer membrane of Gram negatives interacts with Toll-like receptors, especially TLR4, on monocyte/macrophage lineage cells and endothelial cells, activating inflammatory and coagulation pathways and changing endothelial integrity; endotoxin in the blood causes systemic immune activation, the main reason people get very sick. Step 2: Gram positives have no endotoxin; lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycans stimulate similar responses through different TLRs.","w":["Gram positives do not have endotoxin.","Lipoteichoic acid acts through different TLRs.","LPS acts on monocytes/macrophages and endothelial cells.","The innate system is very sensitive to LPS."],"k":"Gram negative LPS → TLR4 → systemic inflammation and clotting.","rn":[["","Why do people get very sick with Gram negative infections?","Why do people get very sick with Gram negative infections?","Endotoxin in blood; Systemic immune activation"]]},{"id":39130,"t":"Staphylococcus aureus","s":"A strain of S. aureus causes a severe skin infection that destroys immune cells (polymorphs) in the lesion. Another strain causes widespread blistering by attacking the skin's structural proteins. A third causes massive T cell activation.<br><br>Which toxins are responsible, in order?","o":["PVL; exfoliative toxin; enterotoxin (superantigen)","Exfoliative toxin; PVL; coagulase","Protein A; capsule; adhesins","Enterotoxin (superantigen); PVL; exfoliative toxin (protease)","Coagulase; enterotoxin; protein A"],"e":"Step 1: Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) is a pore-forming cytotoxin that lyses polymorphs; exfoliative toxins are proteases targeting epidermal structural proteins. Step 2: enterotoxins act as superantigens, stimulating massive T cell activation and immune evasion. Many of these toxins are phage encoded, so only some strains have them.","w":["The first two are swapped, and coagulase is not a superantigen.","These are surface factors for evasion and adherence, not these toxins.","The order is wrong.","Coagulase causes clotting; protein A binds IgG Fc."],"k":"PVL lyses polymorphs; ET attacks epidermis; enterotoxin = superantigen.","rn":[["","What do enterotoxins (superantigens) stimulate?","What do enterotoxins (superantigens) stimulate?","Massive T cell activation; Immune evasion"],["","What do exfoliative toxins target?","What do exfoliative toxins target?","Epidermal structural proteins"],["","What are exfoliative toxins?","What are exfoliative toxins?","Proteases"]]}]);
